(typeof module==="object"&&module.exports?require("../curriculum.js"):BodyguardCurriculum).register(3,[["q3-9be9555e70c0","s3.0-02c1bc461b9d","3.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthird stage of mitosis (and meiosis), during which sister chromatids separate into two new nuclear regions of a dividing cell","anaphase",["cytoplasm","nucleus","cell membrane"],"anaphase: third stage of mitosis (and meiosis), during which sister chromatids separate into two new nuclear regions of a dividing cell.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fig-ch03_00_01","f-7cca7e796811"],["q3-365f59f0d1ce","s3.0-58a5049f49f8","3.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlife cycle of a single cell, from its birth until its division into two new daughter cells","cell cycle",["cell membrane","DNA replication","nucleus"],"cell cycle: life cycle of a single cell, from its birth until its division into two new daughter cells.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","eip-id1169786620263","f-b7dff7d31bb4"],["q3-5820cf6177e6","s3.0-2bd29a254917","3.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane surrounding all animal cells, composed of a lipid bilayer interspersed with various molecules; also known as plasma membrane","cell membrane",["DNA replication","cell cycle","cytoplasm"],"cell membrane: membrane surrounding all animal cells, composed of a lipid bilayer interspersed with various molecules; also known as plasma membrane.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fig-ch03_00_01","f-e5cb5ad1452b"],["q3-ba7144f5aa44","s3.0-7fa310b1b4ce","3.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ninternal material between the cell membrane and nucleus of a cell, mainly consisting of a water-based fluid called cytosol, within which are all the other organelles and cellular solute and suspended materials","cytoplasm",["anaphase","mitosis","DNA replication"],"cytoplasm: internal material between the cell membrane and nucleus of a cell, mainly consisting of a water-based fluid called cytosol, within which are all the other organelles and cellular solute and suspended materials.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fig-ch03_00_01","f-7af1a2764b60"],["q3-59b5abf7fcca","s3.0-2fbbf4042dd9","3.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprocess of duplicating a molecule of DNA","DNA replication",["cell membrane","cell cycle","cytoplasm"],"DNA replication: process of duplicating a molecule of DNA.","The answer begins with D and has 2 words.","eip-id1169786620263","f-e6e141d2d1e5"],["q3-c5eb554f5ab3","s3.0-4d7fb425b8b1","3.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndivision of genetic material, during which the cell nucleus breaks down and two new, fully functional, nuclei are formed","mitosis",["cytoplasm","anaphase","cell cycle"],"mitosis: division of genetic material, during which the cell nucleus breaks down and two new, fully functional, nuclei are formed.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fig-ch03_00_01","f-77784d41e2a7"],["q3-5778fb326a9f","s3.0-1b6eb811867e","3.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncell’s central organelle; contains the cell’s DNA","nucleus",["mitosis","anaphase","cytoplasm"],"nucleus: cell’s central organelle; contains the cell’s DNA.","The answer begins with N and has 1 word.","eip-id1169786620263","f-7cc52da2016d"],["q3-64b076c41c4e","s3.0-31c1f7fd0552","3.0","Introduction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a term used in biology that refers to a dynamic state of balance within parameters that are compatible with life.","Homeostasis",["mitosis","anaphase","nucleus"],"Homeostasis is a term used in biology that refers to a dynamic state of balance within parameters that are compatible with life.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1530911","f-b2388a4d91c3"],["q3-b3d545d95411","s3.1-b69f165da18f","3.1","Transport across the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nform of transport across the cell membrane that requires input of cellular energy","active transport",["electrical gradient","passive transport","concentration gradient"],"active transport: form of transport across the cell membrane that requires input of cellular energy.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id1667456","f-e72cd3b04986"],["q3-e63064d6b518","s3.1-ec3586e89471","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a molecule that exhibits a difference in polarity between its two ends, resulting in a difference in water solubility","amphipathic",["gene","isotonic","ligand"],"amphipathic: describes a molecule that exhibits a difference in polarity between its two ends, resulting in a difference in water solubility.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id1524241","f-3b6b8b751e87"],["q3-52066bc21658","s3.1-995bea71a013","3.1","Membrane Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane-spanning protein that has an inner pore which allows the passage of one or more substances","channel protein",["integral protein","selective permeability","cell membrane"],"channel protein: membrane-spanning protein that has an inner pore which allows the passage of one or more substances.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1858979","f-3a23fe17a15b"],["q3-9316ab61e5e7","s3.1-f5cc67818ba0","3.1","Cell: Cystic Fibrosis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmall appendage on certain cells formed by microtubules and modified for movement of materials across the cellular surface","cilia",["pinocytosis","organelle","hydrophilic"],"cilia: small appendage on certain cells formed by microtubules and modified for movement of materials across the cellular surface.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1113454","f-dc3938d160f3"],["q3-a0b6859e6c4b","s3.1-cf402379d512","3.1","Passive Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndifference in the concentration of a substance between two regions","concentration gradient",["active transport","facilitated diffusion","peripheral protein"],"concentration gradient: difference in the concentration of a substance between two regions.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1435471","f-f6ff06ecc672"],["q3-b16d94ebe05e","s3.1-9f138d3cd94d","3.1","Passive Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmovement of a substance from an area of higher concentration to one of lower concentration","diffusion",["phagocytosis","hydrophilic","ligand"],"diffusion: movement of a substance from an area of higher concentration to one of lower concentration.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1435471","f-363bc9ea673f"],["q3-62fb9f8374c4","s3.1-6f08349a29d1","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndifference in the electrical charge (potential) between two regions","electrical gradient",["receptor-mediated endocytosis","sodium-potassium pump","passive transport"],"electrical gradient: difference in the electrical charge (potential) between two regions.","The answer begins with E and has 2 words.","fs-id2056316","f-10c25de7c426"],["q3-e17c272afb02","s3.1-1517270ec595","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nimport of material into the cell by formation of a membrane-bound vesicle","endocytosis",["cytoplasm","pinocytosis","organelle"],"endocytosis: import of material into the cell by formation of a membrane-bound vesicle.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1500925","f-93f51b467ea0"],["q3-5642e83eae6b","s3.1-0814b9e8efdb","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nexport of a substance out of a cell by formation of a membrane-bound vesicle","exocytosis",["osmosis","pinocytosis","organelle"],"exocytosis: export of a substance out of a cell by formation of a membrane-bound vesicle.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1171152","f-2e94b4015d42"],["q3-760c9563cfc4","s3.1-45b00dfa7070","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfluid exterior to cells; includes the interstitial fluid, blood plasma, and fluid found in other reservoirs in the body","extracellular fluid (ECF)",["intracellular fluid (ICF)","interstitial fluid (IF)","cell membrane"],"extracellular fluid (ECF): fluid exterior to cells; includes the interstitial fluid, blood plasma, and fluid found in other reservoirs in the body.","The answer begins with E and has 3 words.","fs-id2035346","f-7a73a1736a5c"],["q3-eb6c8daa4715","s3.1-fcaa431fcaa0","3.1","The Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndiffusion of a substance with the aid of a membrane protein","facilitated diffusion",["concentration gradient","passive transport","selective permeability"],"facilitated diffusion: diffusion of a substance with the aid of a membrane protein.","The answer begins with F and has 2 words.","fs-id1126733","f-ded220d3c62d"],["q3-efdb976b2a9a","s3.1-bdc8e6790aae","3.1","Cell: Cystic Fibrosis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfunctional length of DNA that provides the genetic information necessary to build a protein","gene",["glycoprotein","endocytosis","osmosis"],"gene: functional length of DNA that provides the genetic information necessary to build a protein.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id1495301","f-caeec4773ea9"],["q3-1a91f73ed5fd","s3.1-e2baa36190cd","3.1","Membrane Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncoating of sugar molecules that surrounds the cell membrane","glycocalyx",["glycoprotein","diffusion","pinocytosis"],"glycocalyx: coating of sugar molecules that surrounds the cell membrane.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id1542775","f-36dde0a8bc8d"],["q3-bf0d044975ef","s3.1-1f7dd85906f7","3.1","Membrane Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprotein that has one or more carbohydrates attached","glycoprotein",["diffusion","hypotonic","exocytosis"],"glycoprotein: protein that has one or more carbohydrates attached.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id1542775","f-ce7ec5306eef"],["q3-9a477968a745","s3.1-27c916a98eec","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a substance or structure attracted to water","hydrophilic",["cilia","hydrophobic","receptor"],"hydrophilic: describes a substance or structure attracted to water.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1524241","f-d0d6bc70f0a0"],["q3-dbf73fe9fd25","s3.1-8db100605c26","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a substance or structure repelled by water","hydrophobic",["glycoprotein","gene","amphipathic"],"hydrophobic: describes a substance or structure repelled by water.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1524241","f-39dda5a9836e"],["q3-fb159517f008","s3.1-82ca10cdde81","3.1","The Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a solution concentration that is higher than a reference concentration","hypertonic",["glycocalyx","cilia","organelle"],"hypertonic: describes a solution concentration that is higher than a reference concentration.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fig-ch03_01_06","f-4466869e442e"],["q3-c0585348811c","s3.1-7d7712afdcae","3.1","The Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a solution concentration that is lower than a reference concentration","hypotonic",["cilia","phagocytosis","hypertonic"],"hypotonic: describes a solution concentration that is lower than a reference concentration.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1237011","f-46e98c079da8"],["q3-247788833457","s3.1-082706f67b84","3.1","Membrane Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane-associated protein that spans the entire width of the lipid bilayer","integral protein",["active transport","facilitated diffusion","selective permeability"],"integral protein: membrane-associated protein that spans the entire width of the lipid bilayer.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id1858979","f-155a34034376"],["q3-759d38910e57","s3.1-1d4416393449","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfluid in the small spaces between cells not contained within blood vessels","interstitial fluid (IF)",["intracellular fluid (ICF)","extracellular fluid (ECF)","glycoprotein"],"interstitial fluid (IF): fluid in the small spaces between cells not contained within blood vessels.","The answer begins with I and has 3 words.","fs-id2035346","f-ed23da910378"],["q3-388947a233ef","s3.1-48830d8d7187","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfluid in the cytosol of cells","intracellular fluid (ICF)",["extracellular fluid (ECF)","interstitial fluid (IF)","integral protein"],"intracellular fluid (ICF): fluid in the cytosol of cells.","The answer begins with I and has 3 words.","fs-id2035346","f-24b443f81a31"],["q3-e9cefbe33da8","s3.1-530faf42f1a1","3.1","The Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a solution concentration that is the same as a reference concentration","isotonic",["gene","amphipathic","glycoprotein"],"isotonic: describes a solution concentration that is the same as a reference concentration.","The answer begins with I and has 1 word.","fs-id1211584","f-bbde5fcd00f5"],["q3-b64de5429815","s3.1-dd13426e6772","3.1","Membrane Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmolecule that binds with specificity to a specific receptor molecule","ligand",["vesicle","glycocalyx","cilia"],"ligand: molecule that binds with specificity to a specific receptor molecule.","The answer begins with L and has 1 word.","fs-id1417839","f-6ae86f61e549"],["q3-6012785bad76","s3.1-9e9734f5c223","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nany of several different types of membrane-enclosed specialized structures in the cell that perform specific functions for the cell","organelle",["isotonic","receptor","diffusion"],"organelle: any of several different types of membrane-enclosed specialized structures in the cell that perform specific functions for the cell.","The answer begins with O and has 1 word.","fs-id1500925","f-13b92d866025"],["q3-53657c9597a1","s3.1-44de1ca5a5d2","3.1","The Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndiffusion of water molecules down their concentration gradient across a selectively permeable membrane","osmosis",["receptor","isotonic","phagocytosis"],"osmosis: diffusion of water molecules down their concentration gradient across a selectively permeable membrane.","The answer begins with O and has 1 word.","fs-id1506843","f-dabe2571c84b"],["q3-1f7044b6f2af","s3.1-94a56033f3d0","3.1","Transport across the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nform of transport across the cell membrane that does not require input of cellular energy","passive transport",["integral protein","electrical gradient","active transport"],"passive transport: form of transport across the cell membrane that does not require input of cellular energy.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id1667456","f-065aa1955f60"],["q3-7f0293a60444","s3.1-734f0211b376","3.1","Membrane Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane-associated protein that does not span the width of the lipid bilayer, but is attached peripherally to integral proteins, membrane lipids, or other components of the membrane","peripheral protein",["facilitated diffusion","selective permeability","sodium-potassium pump"],"peripheral protein: membrane-associated protein that does not span the width of the lipid bilayer, but is attached peripherally to integral proteins, membrane lipids, or other components of the membrane.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id1858979","f-e4ce2f6b3235"],["q3-cee6c03c4c4f","s3.1-e4f96b0cf821","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nendocytosis of large particles","phagocytosis",["hydrophilic","hydrophobic","vesicle"],"phagocytosis: endocytosis of large particles.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1500925","f-c8b17aca7912"],["q3-3f3b1b0f98f9","s3.1-382d59a1422e","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nendocytosis of fluid","pinocytosis",["hypotonic","diffusion","isotonic"],"pinocytosis: endocytosis of fluid.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1500925","f-29d491819e81"],["q3-449cf7899a7c","s3.1-930ae6f5c338","3.1","Membrane Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprotein molecule that contains a binding site for another specific molecule (called a ligand)","receptor",["amphipathic","endocytosis","cilia"],"receptor: protein molecule that contains a binding site for another specific molecule (called a ligand).","The answer begins with R and has 1 word.","fs-id1417839","f-4e62d85e0fda"],["q3-eabb243ece0d","s3.1-edba5553b1f9","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nendocytosis of ligands attached to membrane-bound receptors","receptor-mediated endocytosis",["passive transport","facilitated diffusion","cell membrane"],"receptor-mediated endocytosis: endocytosis of ligands attached to membrane-bound receptors.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fig-ch03_01_09","f-c614ecfa2995"],["q3-2b1d11e39f0d","s3.1-bc5b2db648b3","3.1","Transport across the Cell Membrane","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfeature of any barrier that allows certain substances to cross but excludes others","selective permeability",["channel protein","sodium-potassium pump","facilitated diffusion"],"selective permeability: feature of any barrier that allows certain substances to cross but excludes others.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1667456","f-40e295a6d90e"],["q3-9927af8fe54c","s3.1-219a2f4a346f","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\n(also, Na + /K + ATP-ase) membrane-embedded protein pump that uses ATP to move Na + out of a cell and K + into the cell","sodium-potassium pump",["active transport","selective permeability","cell membrane"],"sodium-potassium pump: (also, Na + /K + ATP-ase) membrane-embedded protein pump that uses ATP to move Na + out of a cell and K + into the cell.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2056316","f-a3facdb13cb6"],["q3-287c6f5cee90","s3.1-efa039c12606","3.1","Active Transport","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane-bound structure that contains materials within or outside of the cell","vesicle",["hypotonic","pinocytosis","exocytosis"],"vesicle: membrane-bound structure that contains materials within or outside of the cell.","The answer begins with V and has 1 word.","fs-id1500925","f-c88bd8421a7f"],["q3-65ce8301f2e1","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDespite differences in structure and function, all living cells in multicellular organisms have a surrounding ____.","cell membrane",["channel protein","facilitated diffusion","sodium-potassium pump"],"Despite differences in structure and function, all living cells in multicellular organisms have a surrounding cell membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1455946","f-db8926997fc0"],["q3-b70875b17917","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is an extremely pliable structure composed primarily of back-to-back phospholipids (a “bilayer”).","cell membrane",["facilitated diffusion","passive transport","electrical gradient"],"The cell membrane is an extremely pliable structure composed primarily of back-to-back phospholipids (a “bilayer”).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1859725","f-f866de8c4877"],["q3-62ff0928ecd7","s3.1-27c916a98eec","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn amphipathic molecule is one that contains both a ____ and a hydrophobic region.","hydrophilic",["ligand","glycoprotein","glycocalyx"],"An amphipathic molecule is one that contains both a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic region.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1524241","f-8d8d7b141d52"],["q3-2e50488098c7","s3.1-27c916a98eec","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ portion can dissolve in water while the hydrophobic portion can trap grease in micelles that then can be washed away.","hydrophilic",["vesicle","hypertonic","diffusion"],"The hydrophilic portion can dissolve in water while the hydrophobic portion can trap grease in micelles that then can be washed away.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1524241","f-547dad275528"],["q3-115312091e85","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ consists of two adjacent layers of phospholipids.","cell membrane",["electrical gradient","passive transport","integral protein"],"The cell membrane consists of two adjacent layers of phospholipids.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2035346","f-6b51316b98c4"],["q3-46413c22c031","s3.1-48830d8d7187","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the fluid interior of the cell.","intracellular fluid (ICF)",["interstitial fluid (IF)","extracellular fluid (ECF)","channel protein"],"Intracellular fluid (ICF) is the fluid interior of the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2035346","f-93f2b8b6860c"],["q3-367826cd14fb","s3.1-45b00dfa7070","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the fluid environment outside the enclosure of the cell membrane.","extracellular fluid (ECF)",["intracellular fluid (ICF)","interstitial fluid (IF)","receptor"],"Extracellular fluid (ECF) is the fluid environment outside the enclosure of the cell membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2035346","f-b6daebf2f2ae"],["q3-5218944d5fe0","s3.1-1d4416393449","3.1","Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the term given to extracellular fluid not contained within blood vessels.","interstitial fluid (IF)",["intracellular fluid (ICF)","extracellular fluid (ECF)","electrical gradient"],"Interstitial fluid (IF) is the term given to extracellular fluid not contained within blood vessels.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2035346","f-f6052e6e62ba"],["q3-ebad15986b54","s3.1-734f0211b376","3.1","Membrane Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTwo different types of proteins that are commonly associated with the cell membrane are the integral proteins and ____.","peripheral protein",["concentration gradient","passive transport","integral protein"],"Two different types of proteins that are commonly associated with the cell membrane are the integral proteins and peripheral protein.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1858979","f-83862788ebbe"],["q3-01c99553e5fd","s3.1-930ae6f5c338","3.1","Membrane Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ligand is the specific molecule that binds to and activates a ____.","receptor",["diffusion","glycocalyx","isotonic"],"A ligand is the specific molecule that binds to and activates a receptor.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1417839","f-0a4927cb12ab"],["q3-afd2c098765f","s3.1-930ae6f5c338","3.1","Membrane Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSome integral proteins serve dual roles as both a ____ and an ion channel.","receptor",["gene","cilia","hydrophilic"],"Some integral proteins serve dual roles as both a receptor and an ion channel.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1417839","f-9df292089719"],["q3-8d681bc294dc","s3.1-930ae6f5c338","3.1","Membrane Proteins","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhen a dopamine molecule binds to a dopamine ____ protein, a channel within the transmembrane protein opens to allow certain ions to flow into the cell.","receptor",["amphipathic","gene","hydrophilic"],"When a dopamine molecule binds to a dopamine receptor protein, a channel within the transmembrane protein opens to allow certain ions to flow into the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1417839","f-100173922885"],["q3-1b67f9c85dfc","s3.1-1f7dd85906f7","3.1","Membrane Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a protein that has carbohydrate molecules attached, which extend into the extracellular matrix.","glycoprotein",["vesicle","exocytosis","cilia"],"A glycoprotein is a protein that has carbohydrate molecules attached, which extend into the extracellular matrix.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1542775","f-e026bdfbf357"],["q3-52e8f8f44b8c","s3.1-e2baa36190cd","3.1","Membrane Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe carbohydrates that extend from membrane proteins and even from some membrane lipids collectively form the ____.","glycocalyx",["cytoplasm","organelle","pinocytosis"],"The carbohydrates that extend from membrane proteins and even from some membrane lipids collectively form the glycocalyx.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1542775","f-6d7ca572e1b3"],["q3-88db9e9df67c","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","Membrane Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe glycocalyx is a fuzzy-appearing coating around the cell formed from glycoproteins and other carbohydrates attached to the ____.","cell membrane",["integral protein","selective permeability","sodium-potassium pump"],"The glycocalyx is a fuzzy-appearing coating around the cell formed from glycoproteins and other carbohydrates attached to the cell membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1542775","f-6faa624bc1fc"],["q3-6c46d662a905","s3.1-d4efebdf2050","3.1","Membrane Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ are typically found on the inner or outer surface of the lipid bilayer but can also be attached to the internal or external surface of an integral protein.","Peripheral proteins",["passive transport","channel protein","selective permeability"],"Peripheral proteins are typically found on the inner or outer surface of the lipid bilayer but can also be attached to the internal or external surface of an integral protein.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1955600","f-879001c1ee55"],["q3-33ffead6f12c","s3.1-d4efebdf2050","3.1","Membrane Proteins","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSome ____ on the surface of intestinal cells, for example, act as digestive enzymes to break down nutrients to sizes that can pass through the cells and into the bloodstream.","Peripheral proteins",["sodium-potassium pump","electrical gradient","channel protein"],"Some peripheral proteins on the surface of intestinal cells, for example, act as digestive enzymes to break down nutrients to sizes that can pass through the cells and into the bloodstream.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1955600","f-5371473530ef"],["q3-bff27738eef9","s3.1-8db100605c26","3.1","Transport across the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe phospholipids are tightly packed together, and the membrane has a ____ interior.","hydrophobic",["hydrophilic","pinocytosis","glycocalyx"],"The phospholipids are tightly packed together, and the membrane has a hydrophobic interior.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1667456","f-c927a0947ee0"],["q3-37e55d55bb43","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","Transport across the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the case of the ____, only relatively small, nonpolar materials can move through the lipid bilayer (remember, the lipid tails of the membrane are nonpolar).","cell membrane",["peripheral protein","concentration gradient","electrical gradient"],"In the case of the cell membrane, only relatively small, nonpolar materials can move through the lipid bilayer (remember, the lipid tails of the membrane are nonpolar).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1667456","f-c9f91f7a1a09"],["q3-d5a1a08f024a","s3.1-94a56033f3d0","3.1","Transport across the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the movement of substances across the membrane without the expenditure of cellular energy.","passive transport",["peripheral protein","facilitated diffusion","selective permeability"],"Passive transport is the movement of substances across the membrane without the expenditure of cellular energy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1667456","f-30fea7ab836e"],["q3-e3ed4af080ce","s3.1-b69f165da18f","3.1","Transport across the Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the movement of substances across the membrane using energy from adenosine triphosphate (ATP).","active transport",["peripheral protein","facilitated diffusion","channel protein"],"In contrast, active transport is the movement of substances across the membrane using energy from adenosine triphosphate (ATP).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1667456","f-b5f9df9fb44d"],["q3-f159256606a8","s3.1-cf402379d512","3.1","Passive Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is the difference in concentration of a substance across a space.","concentration gradient",["active transport","peripheral protein","passive transport"],"A concentration gradient is the difference in concentration of a substance across a space.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1435471","f-02c87e43c5b0"],["q3-ae3f6d2378a2","s3.1-cf402379d512","3.1","Passive Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEventually the sugar will diffuse throughout the tea until no ____ remains.","concentration gradient",["electrical gradient","active transport","channel protein"],"Eventually the sugar will diffuse throughout the tea until no concentration gradient remains.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1435471","f-7bcdce13027f"],["q3-d97cac5dcee9","s3.1-9f138d3cd94d","3.1","Passive Transport","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn both cases, if the room is warmer or the tea hotter, ____ occurs even faster as the molecules are bumping into each other and spreading out faster than at cooler temperatures.","diffusion",["hypotonic","organelle","osmosis"],"In both cases, if the room is warmer or the tea hotter, diffusion occurs even faster as the molecules are bumping into each other and spreading out faster than at cooler temperatures.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1435471","f-baadc74390b9"],["q3-14ee2a6a5ca8","s3.1-9f138d3cd94d","3.1","Passive Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nHaving an internal body temperature around 98.6 ° F thus also aids in ____ of particles within the body.","diffusion",["hydrophobic","osmosis","gene"],"Having an internal body temperature around 98.6 ° F thus also aids in diffusion of particles within the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1435471","f-4cb41b766a4e"],["q3-ab0f91666629","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBefore moving on, you need to review the gases that can diffuse across a ____.","cell membrane",["sodium-potassium pump","channel protein","concentration gradient"],"Before moving on, you need to review the gases that can diffuse across a cell membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1212990","f-b9d3450590fc"],["q3-7b4d1993ee7c","s3.1-66d80f5fad63","3.1","The Cell Membrane","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBecause cells rapidly use up oxygen during metabolism, there is typically a lower ____ of O 2 inside the cell than outside.","concentration",["osmosis","phagocytosis","organelle"],"Because cells rapidly use up oxygen during metabolism, there is typically a lower concentration of O₂ inside the cell than outside.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1212990","f-aebae7b9067d"],["q3-67d196bd24f4","s3.1-7fa310b1b4ce","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs a result, oxygen will diffuse from the interstitial fluid directly through the lipid bilayer of the membrane and into the ____ within the cell.","cytoplasm",["osmosis","cilia","hypertonic"],"As a result, oxygen will diffuse from the interstitial fluid directly through the lipid bilayer of the membrane and into the cytoplasm within the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1212990","f-8ebc67fca4f2"],["q3-0816b0f605b0","s3.1-27c916a98eec","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLarge polar or ionic molecules, which are ____, cannot easily cross the phospholipid bilayer.","hydrophilic",["isotonic","organelle","cytoplasm"],"Large polar or ionic molecules, which are hydrophilic, cannot easily cross the phospholipid bilayer.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1126733","f-2856a4425a27"],["q3-8992f104a251","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCharged atoms or molecules of any size cannot cross the ____ via simple diffusion as the charges are repelled by the hydrophobic tails in the interior of the phospholipid bilayer.","cell membrane",["facilitated diffusion","peripheral protein","concentration gradient"],"Charged atoms or molecules of any size cannot cross the cell membrane via simple diffusion as the charges are repelled by the hydrophobic tails in the interior of the phospholipid bilayer.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1126733","f-bf5d185235eb"],["q3-848a51d64ba2","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWater also can move freely across the ____ of all cells, either through protein channels or by slipping between the lipid tails of the membrane itself.","cell membrane",["sodium-potassium pump","peripheral protein","concentration gradient"],"Water also can move freely across the cell membrane of all cells, either through protein channels or by slipping between the lipid tails of the membrane itself.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1506843","f-8930ef567773"],["q3-0e70288e5c91","s3.1-9f138d3cd94d","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOsmosis is the ____ of water through a semipermeable membrane.","diffusion",["isotonic","exocytosis","ligand"],"Osmosis is the diffusion of water through a semipermeable membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1506843","f-40bd0eaff7b9"],["q3-6bd5b6c5a585","s3.1-530faf42f1a1","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTwo solutions that have the same concentration of solutes are said to be ____ (equal tension).","isotonic",["hydrophobic","receptor","osmosis"],"Two solutions that have the same concentration of solutes are said to be isotonic (equal tension).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1211584","f-e75de48d17e6"],["q3-42ffcfd53cd1","s3.1-44de1ca5a5d2","3.1","The Cell Membrane","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ occurs when there is an imbalance of solutes outside of a cell versus inside the cell.","Osmosis",["receptor","hypotonic","amphipathic"],"Osmosis occurs when there is an imbalance of solutes outside of a cell versus inside the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1237011","f-4db409c4a18d"],["q3-df97824614b4","s3.1-82ca10cdde81","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCells in a ____ solution will shrivel as water leaves the cell via osmosis.","hypertonic",["vesicle","cilia","hydrophobic"],"Cells in a hypertonic solution will shrivel as water leaves the cell via osmosis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1237011","f-e8736249f65c"],["q3-5c096623f825","s3.1-7d7712afdcae","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCells in a ____ solution will take on too much water and swell, with the risk of eventually bursting.","hypotonic",["organelle","pinocytosis","isotonic"],"Cells in a hypotonic solution will take on too much water and swell, with the risk of eventually bursting.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1237011","f-449ed76ae711"],["q3-960aea3833f4","s3.1-530faf42f1a1","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA critical aspect of homeostasis in living things is to create an internal environment in which all of the body’s cells are in an ____ solution.","isotonic",["diffusion","hydrophobic","exocytosis"],"A critical aspect of homeostasis in living things is to create an internal environment in which all of the body’s cells are in an isotonic solution.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1237011","f-c2f973dcf75c"],["q3-951ecafcdbb0","s3.1-f8910c1eb24b","3.1","The Cell Membrane","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is an extremely important process in the body.","Filtration",["amphipathic","cytoplasm","hydrophobic"],"Filtration is an extremely important process in the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1307738","f-d66185b35d61"],["q3-69d74ccbb1c3","s3.1-94a56033f3d0","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMembrane proteins that aid in the ____ of substances do so without the use of ATP.","passive transport",["selective permeability","facilitated diffusion","integral protein"],"Membrane proteins that aid in the passive transport of substances do so without the use of ATP.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1666810","f-d2282e802db0"],["q3-1e61355c8b61","s3.1-b69f165da18f","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOne of the most common types of ____ involves proteins that serve as pumps.","active transport",["receptor-mediated endocytosis","sodium-potassium pump","concentration gradient"],"One of the most common types of active transport involves proteins that serve as pumps.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1465738","f-6f772e8cee4c"],["q3-f7239b70dd2e","s3.1-219a2f4a346f","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____, which is also called Na + /K + ATPase, transports sodium out of a cell while moving potassium into the cell.","sodium-potassium pump",["cell membrane","concentration gradient","electrical gradient"],"The sodium-potassium pump, which is also called Na + /K + ATPase, transports sodium out of a cell while moving potassium into the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2056316","f-85fdc226877f"],["q3-6886736f0c64","s3.1-6f08349a29d1","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is a difference in electrical charge across a space.","electrical gradient",["selective permeability","concentration gradient","cell membrane"],"An electrical gradient is a difference in electrical charge across a space.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2056316","f-9553a3791219"],["q3-93a985c7d1fb","s3.1-6f08349a29d1","3.1","Active Transport","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the case of nerve cells, for example, the ____ exists between the inside and outside of the cell, with the inside being negatively-charged (at around -70 mV) relative to the outside.","electrical gradient",["receptor-mediated endocytosis","concentration gradient","active transport"],"In the case of nerve cells, for example, the electrical gradient exists between the inside and outside of the cell, with the inside being negatively-charged (at around -70 mV) relative to the outside.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2056316","f-4f997092e0b6"],["q3-63df63c18d48","s3.1-6f08349a29d1","3.1","Active Transport","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe negative ____ is maintained because each Na + /K + pump moves three Na + ions out of the cell and two K + ions into the cell for each ATP molecule that is used.","electrical gradient",["receptor-mediated endocytosis","integral protein","facilitated diffusion"],"The negative electrical gradient is maintained because each Na + /K + pump moves three Na + ions out of the cell and two K + ions into the cell for each ATP molecule that is used.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2056316","f-a773d0cc4969"],["q3-f9d7361c4dcd","s3.1-94a56033f3d0","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nActive transport pumps can also work together with other active or ____ systems to move substances across the membrane.","passive transport",["facilitated diffusion","peripheral protein","integral protein"],"Active transport pumps can also work together with other active or passive transport systems to move substances across the membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-152","f-b7b175d9605a"],["q3-1ca7ba61c048","s3.1-219a2f4a346f","3.1","Active Transport","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ maintains a high concentration of sodium ions outside of the cell.","sodium-potassium pump",["peripheral protein","channel protein","active transport"],"For example, the sodium-potassium pump maintains a high concentration of sodium ions outside of the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-152","f-4380e56a6ea5"],["q3-37afe4807152","s3.1-66d80f5fad63","3.1","Active Transport","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBecause cells store glucose for energy, glucose is typically at a higher ____ inside of the cell than outside.","concentration",["hydrophobic","hypotonic","vesicle"],"Because cells store glucose for energy, glucose is typically at a higher concentration inside of the cell than outside.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-214","f-3f7ce4541f35"],["q3-247cf6ff260f","s3.1-219a2f4a346f","3.1","Active Transport","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDue to the action of the ____, sodium ions will easily diffuse into the cell when the symporter is opened.","sodium-potassium pump",["concentration gradient","passive transport","electrical gradient"],"However, due to the action of the sodium-potassium pump, sodium ions will easily diffuse into the cell when the symporter is opened.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-214","f-24fcc8a92853"],["q3-335a52cf2b23","s3.1-b69f165da18f","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nConversely, antiporters are secondary ____ systems that transport substances in opposite directions.","active transport",["passive transport","channel protein","integral protein"],"Conversely, antiporters are secondary active transport systems that transport substances in opposite directions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-677","f-da867aa76d07"],["q3-5808b9c6bc7d","s3.1-9e9734f5c223","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA vesicle is a membranous sac—a spherical and hollow ____ bounded by a lipid bilayer membrane.","organelle",["glycoprotein","pinocytosis","hypertonic"],"A vesicle is a membranous sac—a spherical and hollow organelle bounded by a lipid bilayer membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1500925","f-e1d530425a8f"],["q3-376e4712ce07","s3.1-1517270ec595","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ often brings materials into the cell that must be broken down or digested.","Endocytosis",["hydrophilic","cytoplasm","cilia"],"Endocytosis often brings materials into the cell that must be broken down or digested.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1500925","f-3f8d6b57a674"],["q3-f4609c231da6","s3.1-e4f96b0cf821","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ (“cell eating”) is the endocytosis of large particles.","Phagocytosis",["isotonic","vesicle","diffusion"],"Phagocytosis (“cell eating”) is the endocytosis of large particles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1500925","f-f9306096a37b"],["q3-28ff8bcceffd","s3.1-e4f96b0cf821","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMany immune cells engage in ____ of invading pathogens.","phagocytosis",["amphipathic","hypertonic","ligand"],"Many immune cells engage in phagocytosis of invading pathogens.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1500925","f-34651ec4c9ef"],["q3-4ec9ed5c3c66","s3.1-e4f96b0cf821","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTo ____, pinocytosis (“cell drinking”) brings fluid containing dissolved substances into a cell through membrane vesicles.","phagocytosis",["osmosis","ligand","endocytosis"],"In contrast to phagocytosis, pinocytosis (“cell drinking”) brings fluid containing dissolved substances into a cell through membrane vesicles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1500925","f-880d7381682f"],["q3-6a1031f14179","s3.1-edba5553b1f9","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCells regulate the endocytosis of specific substances via ____.","receptor-mediated endocytosis",["passive transport","concentration gradient","cell membrane"],"Cells regulate the endocytosis of specific substances via receptor-mediated endocytosis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1097508","f-9b3e573de410"],["q3-b9a5ef6a742a","s3.1-edba5553b1f9","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is endocytosis by a portion of the cell membrane that contains many receptors that are specific for a certain substance.","receptor-mediated endocytosis",["sodium-potassium pump","channel protein","concentration gradient"],"Receptor-mediated endocytosis is endocytosis by a portion of the cell membrane that contains many receptors that are specific for a certain substance.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1097508","f-bad6e2669653"],["q3-0276c3bb80da","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOnce the surface receptors have bound sufficient amounts of the specific substance (the receptor’s ligand), the cell will endocytose the part of the ____ containing the receptor-ligand complexes.","cell membrane",["selective permeability","electrical gradient","sodium-potassium pump"],"Once the surface receptors have bound sufficient amounts of the specific substance (the receptor’s ligand), the cell will endocytose the part of the cell membrane containing the receptor-ligand complexes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1097508","f-09e6ffcf3494"],["q3-1758fd6d574f","s3.1-601d9729a0d8","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIron is bound to a protein called ____ in the blood.","transferrin",["cilia","organelle","hypertonic"],"Iron is bound to a protein called transferrin in the blood.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1097508","f-03266c9d1323"],["q3-0c35fe00535f","s3.1-930ae6f5c338","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSpecific transferrin receptors on red blood cell surfaces bind the iron-transferrin molecules, and the cell endocytoses the ____-ligand complexes.","receptor",["vesicle","osmosis","exocytosis"],"Specific transferrin receptors on red blood cell surfaces bind the iron-transferrin molecules, and the cell endocytoses the receptor-ligand complexes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1097508","f-67e4607f39db"],["q3-90d309da1cc8","s3.1-1517270ec595","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWith ____, exocytosis (taking “out of the cell”) is the process of a cell exporting material using vesicular transport.","endocytosis",["receptor","cytoplasm","ligand"],"In contrast with endocytosis, exocytosis (taking “out of the cell”) is the process of a cell exporting material using vesicular transport.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1171152","f-46e3c7301fa0"],["q3-f0be8ad970d1","s3.1-2bd29a254917","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe vesicle membrane then becomes part of the ____.","cell membrane",["passive transport","channel protein","electrical gradient"],"The vesicle membrane then becomes part of the cell membrane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1171152","f-53ea6832c1bf"],["q3-096ff2c10bc6","s3.1-0814b9e8efdb","3.1","Active Transport","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCells of the stomach and pancreas produce and secrete digestive enzymes through ____.","exocytosis",["diffusion","hydrophilic","gene"],"Cells of the stomach and pancreas produce and secrete digestive enzymes through exocytosis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1171152","f-0845706e00bb"],["q3-1615496f9829","s3.1-f5cc67818ba0","3.1","Cell: Cystic Fibrosis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA cilium (plural ____) is one of the hair-like appendages found on certain cells.","= cilia",["selective permeability","passive transport","receptor-mediated endocytosis"],"A cilium (plural = cilia) is one of the hair-like appendages found on certain cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1113454","f-7cb64e3165a3"],["q3-ea3fc02e83dc","s3.1-cf402379d512","3.1","Cell: Cystic Fibrosis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe absence of ions in the secreted mucus results in the lack of a normal water ____.","concentration gradient",["sodium-potassium pump","electrical gradient","selective permeability"],"The absence of ions in the secreted mucus results in the lack of a normal water concentration gradient.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1321541","f-ba730c743c83"],["q3-50d56a0d0b20","s3.1-3b4c0c6565e0","3.1","Cell: Cystic Fibrosis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThus, there is no osmotic ____ pulling water into the mucus.","pressure",["diffusion","glycoprotein","osmosis"],"Thus, there is no osmotic pressure pulling water into the mucus.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1321541","f-bc5cc6c1dd64"],["q3-2eac255b0682","s3.2-727462a57822","3.2","Lysosomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nbreakdown of cells by their own enzymatic action","autolysis",["peroxisome","organelle","gene"],"autolysis: breakdown of cells by their own enzymatic action.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2549673","f-137d10f045df"],["q3-c90f314e5dba","s3.2-677025ace176","3.2","Lysosomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlysosomal breakdown of a cell’s own components","autophagy",["ribosome","cytoskeleton","cytosol"],"autophagy: lysosomal breakdown of a cell’s own components.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2027468","f-9220345ae9ca"],["q3-af188cad52a9","s3.2-2d261136de28","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmall, self-replicating organelle that provides the origin for microtubule growth and moves DNA during cell division","centriole",["gene","ribosome","peroxisome"],"centriole: small, self-replicating organelle that provides the origin for microtubule growth and moves DNA during cell division.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1524473","f-08c00fd62149"],["q3-d84fdde01388","s3.2-2400d247aa59","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncontractile ring that forms around a cell during cytokinesis that pinches the cell into two halves","cleavage furrow",["Golgi apparatus","intermediate filament","cell membrane"],"cleavage furrow: contractile ring that forms around a cell during cytokinesis that pinches the cell into two halves.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1094713","f-0fbdd504fcfe"],["q3-00078a89059f","s3.2-b187ad43f313","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\n“skeleton” of a cell; formed by rod-like proteins that support the cell’s shape and provide, among other functions, locomotive abilities","cytoskeleton",["translation","mitochondrion","microfilament"],"cytoskeleton: “skeleton” of a cell; formed by rod-like proteins that support the cell’s shape and provide, among other functions, locomotive abilities.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1521089","f-fb67889e78b5"],["q3-f411416fdc3c","s3.2-2fea29c4388f","3.2","The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nclear, semi-fluid medium of the cytoplasm, made up mostly of water","cytosol",["lysosome","mitochondrion","organelle"],"cytosol: clear, semi-fluid medium of the cytoplasm, made up mostly of water.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2256994","f-37540c9b2aa1"],["q3-cdaf00eed60e","s3.2-163ccf7256ff","3.2","Endoplasmic Reticulum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncellular organelle that consists of interconnected membrane-bound tubules, which may or may not be associated with ribosomes (rough type or smooth type, respectively)","endoplasmic reticulum (ER)",["vesicle","translation","cleavage furrow"],"endoplasmic reticulum (ER): cellular organelle that consists of interconnected membrane-bound tubules, which may or may not be associated with ribosomes (rough type or smooth type, respectively).","The answer begins with E and has 3 words.","fs-id2132190","f-854b46e1fef0"],["q3-66afe364a882","s3.2-fb7d3a4bab74","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nappendage on certain cells formed by microtubules and modified for movement","flagellum",["mitochondrion","translation","lysosome"],"flagellum: appendage on certain cells formed by microtubules and modified for movement.","The answer begins with F and has 1 word.","fs-id704793","f-5cf9e1e393e1"],["q3-c7e845ee4522","s3.2-a5db44d5ad2e","3.2","Organelles of the Endomembrane System","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncellular organelle formed by a series of flattened, membrane-bound sacs that functions in protein modification, tagging, packaging, and transport","Golgi apparatus",["cell membrane","intermediate filament","cleavage furrow"],"Golgi apparatus: cellular organelle formed by a series of flattened, membrane-bound sacs that functions in protein modification, tagging, packaging, and transport.","The answer begins with G and has 2 words.","fs-id1091621","f-37fb38f3194b"],["q3-1fb790661513","s3.2-b936a79a73dc","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntype of cytoskeletal filament made of keratin, characterized by an intermediate thickness, and playing a role in resisting cellular tension","intermediate filament",["cell membrane","cleavage furrow","Golgi apparatus"],"intermediate filament: type of cytoskeletal filament made of keratin, characterized by an intermediate thickness, and playing a role in resisting cellular tension.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id2259822","f-d436c714ca98"],["q3-e1b599a6901e","s3.2-1117596c1a08","3.2","Lysosomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane-bound cellular organelle originating from the Golgi apparatus and containing digestive enzymes","lysosome",["exocytosis","vesicle","cilia"],"lysosome: membrane-bound cellular organelle originating from the Golgi apparatus and containing digestive enzymes.","The answer begins with L and has 1 word.","fs-id2027468","f-21f2a8fca1e9"],["q3-7f7e5638d0ec","s3.2-de1153c1f561","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthe thinnest of the cytoskeletal filaments; composed of actin subunits that function in muscle contraction and cellular structural support","microfilament",["peroxisome","cytoplasm","centriole"],"microfilament: the thinnest of the cytoskeletal filaments; composed of actin subunits that function in muscle contraction and cellular structural support.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1493979","f-4b45c3459534"],["q3-9128e25ccc49","s3.2-c2e472ad718a","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthe thickest of the cytoskeletal filaments, composed of tubulin subunits that function in cellular movement and structural support","microtubule",["flagellum","lysosome","exocytosis"],"microtubule: the thickest of the cytoskeletal filaments, composed of tubulin subunits that function in cellular movement and structural support.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id704793","f-628b4d909e47"],["q3-6082bc1701d3","s3.2-bdacb761ed34","3.2","Mitochondria","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of the cellular organelles bound by a double lipid bilayer that function primarily in the production of cellular energy (ATP)","mitochondrion",["exocytosis","nucleus","translation"],"mitochondrion: one of the cellular organelles bound by a double lipid bilayer that function primarily in the production of cellular energy (ATP).","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1692132","f-6d1c1e6eb383"],["q3-773a0b58a6c1","s3.2-22de9788dbad","3.2","Peroxisomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nchange in the nucleotide sequence in a gene within a cell’s DNA","mutation",["mitochondrion","flagellum","lysosome"],"mutation: change in the nucleotide sequence in a gene within a cell’s DNA.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1179935","f-7f0a6fcf992a"],["q3-2e435184b99f","s3.2-2107ca8f132e","3.2","Peroxisomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane-bound organelle that contains enzymes primarily responsible for detoxifying harmful substances","peroxisome",["exocytosis","nucleus","flagellum"],"peroxisome: membrane-bound organelle that contains enzymes primarily responsible for detoxifying harmful substances.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1503802","f-472b64d42ec0"],["q3-5c6d7b95e7e9","s3.2-88426a49efce","3.2","Peroxisomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\na group of extremely reactive peroxides and oxygen-containing radicals that may contribute to cellular damage","reactive oxygen species (ROS)",["endoplasmic reticulum (ER)","Golgi apparatus","cleavage furrow"],"reactive oxygen species (ROS): a group of extremely reactive peroxides and oxygen-containing radicals that may contribute to cellular damage.","The answer begins with R and has 4 words.","fs-id1863415","f-732da199a39f"],["q3-58b35c818c16","s3.2-018b81a727a4","3.2","Endoplasmic Reticulum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncellular organelle that functions in protein synthesis","ribosome",["microfilament","cilia","nucleus"],"ribosome: cellular organelle that functions in protein synthesis.","The answer begins with R and has 1 word.","fs-id2552695","f-f8824098bdce"],["q3-ec4f1ee2acb8","s3.2-efd7fd7f2954","3.2","Endoplasmic Reticulum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprocess of producing a protein from the nucleotide sequence code of an mRNA transcript","translation",["cilia","centriole","lysosome"],"translation: process of producing a protein from the nucleotide sequence code of an mRNA transcript.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id2552695","f-1b6a0031e238"],["q3-df54ffc928a4","s3.2-7fa310b1b4ce","3.2","The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAll living cells in multicellular organisms contain an internal cytoplasmic compartment, and a nucleus within the ____.","cytoplasm",["cytosol","lysosome","gene"],"All living cells in multicellular organisms contain an internal cytoplasmic compartment, and a nucleus within the cytoplasm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2256994","f-224361af5cca"],["q3-6b917888f6a0","s3.2-2fea29c4388f","3.2","The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____, the jelly-like substance within the cell, provides the fluid medium necessary for biochemical reactions.","Cytosol",["peroxisome","nucleus","gene"],"Cytosol, the jelly-like substance within the cell, provides the fluid medium necessary for biochemical reactions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2256994","f-324c28426cf7"],["q3-5531367bfc4a","s3.2-9e9734f5c223","3.2","The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ (“little organ”) is one of several different types of membrane-enclosed bodies in the cell, each performing a unique function.","organelle",["nucleus","peroxisome","ribosome"],"An organelle (“little organ”) is one of several different types of membrane-enclosed bodies in the cell, each performing a unique function.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2256994","f-55ab14aca6b9"],["q3-2c140d520ab3","s3.2-7fa310b1b4ce","3.2","The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe organelles and cytosol, taken together, compose the cell’s ____.","cytoplasm",["microtubule","autophagy","exocytosis"],"The organelles and cytosol, taken together, compose the cell’s cytoplasm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2256994","f-30185b2a824d"],["q3-a30b048ebc2b","s3.2-9e9734f5c223","3.2","The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe nucleus is a cell’s central ____, which contains the cell’s DNA.","organelle",["autophagy","lysosome","ribosome"],"The nucleus is a cell’s central organelle, which contains the cell’s DNA.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2256994","f-ab37b46df601"],["q3-3b8509375234","s3.2-a5db44d5ad2e","3.2","Organelles of the Endomembrane System","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe organelles of the endomembrane system include the endoplasmic reticulum, ____, and vesicles.","Golgi apparatus",["cell membrane","intermediate filament","cleavage furrow"],"The organelles of the endomembrane system include the endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, and vesicles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1091621","f-3142249fee31"],["q3-186156f1888d","s3.2-163ccf7256ff","3.2","Endoplasmic Reticulum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is a system of channels that is continuous with the nuclear membrane (or “envelope”) covering the nucleus and composed of the same lipid bilayer material.","endoplasmic reticulum (ER)",["cytoplasm","peroxisome","exocytosis"],"The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a system of channels that is continuous with the nuclear membrane (or “envelope”) covering the nucleus and composed of the same lipid bilayer material.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2132190","f-1b451bd4f907"],["q3-06c36915f718","s3.2-9e9734f5c223","3.2","Endoplasmic Reticulum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ribosome is an ____ that serves as the site of protein synthesis.","organelle",["vesicle","cilia","autophagy"],"A ribosome is an organelle that serves as the site of protein synthesis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2552695","f-9169eb6e7eef"],["q3-53a4d22a81ee","s3.2-2bd29a254917","3.2","Endoplasmic Reticulum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWith the smooth ER, the primary job of the rough ER is the synthesis and modification of proteins destined for the ____ or for export from the cell.","cell membrane",["cleavage furrow","intermediate filament","Golgi apparatus"],"In contrast with the smooth ER, the primary job of the rough ER is the synthesis and modification of proteins destined for the cell membrane or for export from the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2581786","f-b55333367c62"],["q3-74b888ccb64e","s3.2-a5db44d5ad2e","3.2","The Golgi Apparatus","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is responsible for sorting, modifying, and shipping off the products that come from the rough ER, much like a post-office.","Golgi apparatus",["cell membrane","intermediate filament","cleavage furrow"],"The Golgi apparatus is responsible for sorting, modifying, and shipping off the products that come from the rough ER, much like a post-office.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484553","f-4bccda8d6db6"],["q3-3a53e1219049","s3.2-a5db44d5ad2e","3.2","The Golgi Apparatus","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ looks like stacked flattened discs, almost like stacks of oddly shaped pancakes.","Golgi apparatus",["cleavage furrow","cell membrane","intermediate filament"],"The Golgi apparatus looks like stacked flattened discs, almost like stacks of oddly shaped pancakes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484553","f-48b4793b8061"],["q3-174409e8b782","s3.2-a5db44d5ad2e","3.2","The Golgi Apparatus","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ has two distinct sides, each with a different role.","Golgi apparatus",["intermediate filament","cleavage furrow","cell membrane"],"The Golgi apparatus has two distinct sides, each with a different role.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484553","f-09da665ac4e8"],["q3-32b7ef7c203a","s3.2-2bd29a254917","3.2","The Golgi Apparatus","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf the product is to be exported from the cell, the vesicle migrates to the cell surface and fuses to the ____, and the cargo is secreted.","cell membrane",["intermediate filament","cleavage furrow","Golgi apparatus"],"If the product is to be exported from the cell, the vesicle migrates to the cell surface and fuses to the cell membrane, and the cargo is secreted.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484553","f-5d754a900131"],["q3-6171919abcb5","s3.2-1117596c1a08","3.2","Lysosomes","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhen certain immune defense cells (white blood cells) phagocytize bacteria, the bacterial cell is transported into a ____ and digested by the enzymes inside.","lysosome",["cytoskeleton","cilia","autophagy"],"For example, when certain immune defense cells (white blood cells) phagocytize bacteria, the bacterial cell is transported into a lysosome and digested by the enzymes inside.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2027468","f-b2c08c9b5bff"],["q3-dbf0781eeb8e","s3.2-bdacb761ed34","3.2","Mitochondria","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ (plural = mitochondria) is a membranous, bean-shaped organelle that is the “energy transformer” of the cell.","mitochondrion",["exocytosis","cilia","mutation"],"A mitochondrion (plural = mitochondria) is a membranous, bean-shaped organelle that is the “energy transformer” of the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1692132","f-e650f5feb08b"],["q3-157adaa5498f","s3.2-2107ca8f132e","3.2","Peroxisomes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLike lysosomes, a ____ is a membrane-bound cellular organelle that contains mostly enzymes.","peroxisome",["autolysis","mutation","vesicle"],"Like lysosomes, a peroxisome is a membrane-bound cellular organelle that contains mostly enzymes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1503802","f-f6e56fab91d9"],["q3-9466fb67d30a","s3.2-22de9788dbad","3.2","Peroxisomes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a change in the nucleotide sequence in a gene within a cell’s DNA, potentially altering the protein coded by that gene.","mutation",["autolysis","lysosome","cytoplasm"],"A mutation is a change in the nucleotide sequence in a gene within a cell’s DNA, potentially altering the protein coded by that gene.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1179935","f-d626b488ab07"],["q3-945a953bdd30","s3.2-b187ad43f313","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ forms a complex thread-like network throughout the cell consisting of three different kinds of protein-based filaments: microfilaments, intermediate filaments, and microtubules.","cytoskeleton",["organelle","mitochondrion","centriole"],"The cytoskeleton forms a complex thread-like network throughout the cell consisting of three different kinds of protein-based filaments: microfilaments, intermediate filaments, and microtubules.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id704793","f-9abb15cbfc90"],["q3-42925c5fe157","s3.2-c2e472ad718a","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe thickest of the three is the ____, a structural filament composed of subunits of a protein called tubulin.","microtubule",["cytosol","flagellum","ribosome"],"The thickest of the three is the microtubule, a structural filament composed of subunits of a protein called tubulin.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id704793","f-041025d9ca0e"],["q3-19fb450105b5","s3.2-f5cc67818ba0","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ are found on many cells of the body, including the epithelial cells that line the airways of the respiratory system.","Cilia",["microtubule","mutation","cytoplasm"],"Cilia are found on many cells of the body, including the epithelial cells that line the airways of the respiratory system.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id704793","f-2bb696bb4e3f"],["q3-eb34062d057e","s3.2-f5cc67818ba0","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBeating ____ on cells in the female fallopian tubes move egg cells from the ovary towards the uterus.","cilia",["organelle","mutation","cytosol"],"Beating cilia on cells in the female fallopian tubes move egg cells from the ovary towards the uterus.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id704793","f-77ac14153c28"],["q3-20dd6afdecec","s3.2-fb7d3a4bab74","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ (plural = flagella) is an appendage larger than a cilium and specialized for cell locomotion.","flagellum",["translation","mutation","mitochondrion"],"A flagellum (plural = flagella) is an appendage larger than a cilium and specialized for cell locomotion.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id704793","f-a3986fbc420d"],["q3-b0a174dcbd16","s3.2-c2e472ad718a","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTwo short, identical ____ structures called centrioles are found near the nucleus of cells.","microtubule",["cytosol","gene","cilia"],"Two short, identical microtubule structures called centrioles are found near the nucleus of cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1524473","f-2e8dae8c8fd8"],["q3-f38bd475e08d","s3.2-2d261136de28","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ can serve as the cellular origin point for microtubules extending outward as cilia or flagella or can assist with the separation of DNA during cell division.","centriole",["cytosol","ribosome","organelle"],"A centriole can serve as the cellular origin point for microtubules extending outward as cilia or flagella or can assist with the separation of DNA during cell division.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1524473","f-fe19074feab9"],["q3-b7089c442cf7","s3.2-de1153c1f561","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWith microtubules, the ____ is a thinner type of cytoskeletal filament (see b ).","microfilament",["translation","peroxisome","lysosome"],"In contrast with microtubules, the microfilament is a thinner type of cytoskeletal filament (see b ).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1493979","f-8298982c5b9f"],["q3-e821f5782d44","s3.2-2400d247aa59","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhen a cell is about to split in half during cell division, actin filaments work with myosin to create a ____ that eventually splits the cell down the middle, forming two new cells from the original cell.","cleavage furrow",["Golgi apparatus","intermediate filament","cell membrane"],"When a cell is about to split in half during cell division, actin filaments work with myosin to create a cleavage furrow that eventually splits the cell down the middle, forming two new cells from the original cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1094713","f-45622a5bc86c"],["q3-6060a101f6ed","s3.2-19b4105fb6ab","3.2","The Cytoskeleton","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ help anchor organelles together within a cell and also link cells to other cells by forming special cell-to-cell junctions.","Intermediate filaments",["Golgi apparatus","cell membrane","cleavage furrow"],"Intermediate filaments help anchor organelles together within a cell and also link cells to other cells by forming special cell-to-cell junctions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2259822","f-56546bb1ef57"],["q3-edb6ecdb9a49","s3.3-acbde35ce843","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubstance consisting of DNA and associated proteins","chromatin",["gene","histone","chromosome"],"chromatin: substance consisting of DNA and associated proteins.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1954259","f-90a296caa095"],["q3-6874f460e5b3","s3.3-17e860b0f90a","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncondensed version of chromatin","chromosome",["gene","cytoplasm","nucleus"],"chromosome: condensed version of chromatin.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1954259","f-9679e4fb4901"],["q3-d3c8135da98e","s3.3-95d740552854","3.3","DNA Replication","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nenzyme that functions in adding new nucleotides to a growing strand of DNA during DNA replication","DNA polymerase",["nuclear envelope","nuclear pore","helicase"],"DNA polymerase: enzyme that functions in adding new nucleotides to a growing strand of DNA during DNA replication.","The answer begins with D and has 2 words.","fs-id1448707","f-7f5203592554"],["q3-eeff2cca5937","s3.3-a5c9b737709f","3.3","DNA Replication","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nentire complement of an organism’s DNA; found within virtually every cell","genome",["chromatin","gene","nucleosome"],"genome: entire complement of an organism’s DNA; found within virtually every cell.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fig-ch03_03_06","f-92efecd93baf"],["q3-0a98c0aea933","s3.3-01beb7bf51d9","3.3","DNA Replication","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nenzyme that functions to separate the two DNA strands of a double helix during DNA replication","helicase",["nucleosome","histone","cytoplasm"],"helicase: enzyme that functions to separate the two DNA strands of a double helix during DNA replication.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1436027","f-779ae1fd40b7"],["q3-800af4fb3b2a","s3.3-4728ffbecd47","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfamily of proteins that associate with DNA in the nucleus to form chromatin","histone",["cytoplasm","nucleolus","helicase"],"histone: family of proteins that associate with DNA in the nucleus to form chromatin.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1954259","f-ae114eeeea6b"],["q3-9e8c47223b09","s3.3-d3ea8584b6cf","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane that surrounds the nucleus; consisting of a double lipid-bilayer","nuclear envelope",["DNA replication","DNA polymerase","nuclear pore"],"nuclear envelope: membrane that surrounds the nucleus; consisting of a double lipid-bilayer.","The answer begins with N and has 2 words.","fs-id1484999","f-f0f65b0e24d1"],["q3-b3311f0c387f","s3.3-23d8eaa39190","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of the small, protein-lined openings found scattered throughout the nuclear envelope","nuclear pore",["DNA polymerase","DNA replication","nucleus"],"nuclear pore: one of the small, protein-lined openings found scattered throughout the nuclear envelope.","The answer begins with N and has 2 words.","fs-id1484999","f-6af0866d3c32"],["q3-9852b24b0811","s3.3-e295197f31bb","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmall region of the nucleus that functions in ribosome synthesis","nucleolus",["nucleosome","chromosome","histone"],"nucleolus: small region of the nucleus that functions in ribosome synthesis.","The answer begins with N and has 1 word.","fs-id1988147","f-94f489d1949b"],["q3-2c2707cc538d","s3.3-b969bf6a00c2","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nunit of chromatin consisting of a DNA strand wrapped around histone proteins","nucleosome",["cytoplasm","nucleolus","nucleus"],"nucleosome: unit of chromatin consisting of a DNA strand wrapped around histone proteins.","The answer begins with N and has 1 word.","fs-id1954259","f-582b4c5dac34"],["q3-41759b041e9e","s3.3-1b6eb811867e","3.3","The Nucleus and DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the largest and most prominent of a cell’s organelles.","nucleus",["histone","nucleolus","chromatin"],"The nucleus is the largest and most prominent of a cell’s organelles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1955355","f-bc7b555c6dc7"],["q3-3c68f6b402f5","s3.3-1b6eb811867e","3.3","The Nucleus and DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ sends “commands” to the cell via molecular messengers that translate the information from DNA.","nucleus",["helicase","gene","nucleolus"],"The nucleus sends “commands” to the cell via molecular messengers that translate the information from DNA.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1715438","f-3203564ac4f2"],["q3-7f33e8795bd2","s3.3-23d8eaa39190","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a tiny passageway for the passage of proteins, RNA, and solutes between the nucleus and the cytoplasm.","nuclear pore",["DNA polymerase","nuclear envelope","DNA replication"],"A nuclear pore is a tiny passageway for the passage of proteins, RNA, and solutes between the nucleus and the cytoplasm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484999","f-3be749b8dee7"],["q3-3f2511c8ef0e","s3.3-1b6eb811867e","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nProteins called pore complexes lining the nuclear pores regulate the passage of materials into and out of the ____.","nucleus",["nucleosome","cytoplasm","nucleolus"],"Proteins called pore complexes lining the nuclear pores regulate the passage of materials into and out of the nucleus.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484999","f-2e8b1b06048c"],["q3-2a906e3d7c73","s3.3-d3ea8584b6cf","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nInside the ____ is a gel-like nucleoplasm with solutes that include the building blocks of nucleic acids.","nuclear envelope",["nuclear pore","DNA polymerase","DNA replication"],"Inside the nuclear envelope is a gel-like nucleoplasm with solutes that include the building blocks of nucleic acids.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1988147","f-250fcfae5824"],["q3-07c549cf0b5d","s3.3-e295197f31bb","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThere also can be a dark-staining mass often visible under a simple light microscope, called a ____ (plural = nucleoli).","nucleolus",["cytoplasm","helicase","histone"],"There also can be a dark-staining mass often visible under a simple light microscope, called a nucleolus (plural = nucleoli).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1988147","f-c2f7a96984c0"],["q3-9cfa5543b611","s3.3-e295197f31bb","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is a region of the nucleus that is responsible for manufacturing the RNA necessary for construction of ribosomes.","nucleolus",["chromosome","genome","histone"],"The nucleolus is a region of the nucleus that is responsible for manufacturing the RNA necessary for construction of ribosomes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1988147","f-01209a8f3d10"],["q3-52a996ae41af","s3.3-1b6eb811867e","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOnce synthesized, newly made ribosomal subunits exit the cell’s ____ through the nuclear pores.","nucleus",["nucleosome","gene","chromosome"],"Once synthesized, newly made ribosomal subunits exit the cell’s nucleus through the nuclear pores.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1988147","f-d86b8034a0f0"],["q3-2c82e0dceac7","s3.3-acbde35ce843","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWithin the nucleus are threads of ____ composed of DNA and associated proteins.","chromatin",["gene","histone","nucleosome"],"Within the nucleus are threads of chromatin composed of DNA and associated proteins.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1954259","f-02f218dd76c6"],["q3-f5f5fa8ed16d","s3.3-acbde35ce843","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAlong the ____ threads, the DNA is wrapped around a set of histone proteins.","chromatin",["cytoplasm","genome","helicase"],"Along the chromatin threads, the DNA is wrapped around a set of histone proteins.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1954259","f-f7ade1eaef8e"],["q3-fba638fcccca","s3.3-b969bf6a00c2","3.3","Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a single, wrapped DNA-histone complex.","nucleosome",["chromosome","gene","nucleolus"],"A nucleosome is a single, wrapped DNA-histone complex.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1954259","f-c6d4e557d99e"],["q3-83d7b20c6885","s3.3-2fbbf4042dd9","3.3","DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the copying of DNA that occurs before cell division can take place.","DNA replication",["nuclear pore","nuclear envelope","DNA polymerase"],"DNA replication is the copying of DNA that occurs before cell division can take place.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1828664","f-2fb42eb87445"],["q3-cc276dd40edd","s3.3-2fbbf4042dd9","3.3","DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAfter a great deal of debate and experimentation, the general method of ____ was deduced in 1958 by two scientists in California, Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl.","DNA replication",["nuclear pore","nuclear envelope","DNA polymerase"],"After a great deal of debate and experimentation, the general method of DNA replication was deduced in 1958 by two scientists in California, Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1828664","f-e668dd77a7c7"],["q3-384ef362af2a","s3.3-01beb7bf51d9","3.3","DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSpecial enzymes, including ____, untwist and separate the two strands of DNA.","helicase",["nucleosome","chromatin","nucleolus"],"Special enzymes, including helicase, untwist and separate the two strands of DNA.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1436027","f-505135377d1b"],["q3-1909d352195c","s3.3-95d740552854","3.3","DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ brings in the correct bases to complement the template strand, synthesizing a new strand base by base.","DNA polymerase",["DNA replication","nuclear envelope","nuclear pore"],"DNA polymerase brings in the correct bases to complement the template strand, synthesizing a new strand base by base.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1448707","f-7690d11b3c45"],["q3-b94d13ccc0d2","s3.3-95d740552854","3.3","DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is an enzyme that adds free nucleotides to the end of a chain of DNA, making a new double strand.","DNA polymerase",["nuclear envelope","DNA replication","nuclear pore"],"A DNA polymerase is an enzyme that adds free nucleotides to the end of a chain of DNA, making a new double strand.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1448707","f-f79a17b4597b"],["q3-bb860af0171e","s3.3-2fbbf4042dd9","3.3","DNA Replication","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOnce the process of ____ is complete, the cell is ready to divide.","DNA replication",["DNA polymerase","nuclear pore","nuclear envelope"],"Once the process of DNA replication is complete, the cell is ready to divide.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1471472","f-20ef9b6c74d2"],["q3-ae2f56a25020","s3.4-d6f9f075902b","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nconsecutive sequence of three nucleotides on a tRNA molecule that is complementary to a specific codon on an mRNA molecule","anticodon",["nucleus","cytoplasm","polypeptide"],"anticodon: consecutive sequence of three nucleotides on a tRNA molecule that is complementary to a specific codon on an mRNA molecule.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id1205938","f-315ed8aae2e6"],["q3-f82297489aee","s3.4-7402411f9090","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nconsecutive sequence of three nucleotides on an mRNA molecule that corresponds to a specific amino acid","codon",["nucleus","splicing","promoter"],"codon: consecutive sequence of three nucleotides on an mRNA molecule that corresponds to a specific amino acid.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1751681","f-63883c8eb8aa"],["q3-a6642d63b82d","s3.4-058cd4e3c65e","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of the coding regions of an mRNA molecule that remain after splicing","exon",["intron","codon","polyribosome"],"exon: one of the coding regions of an mRNA molecule that remain after splicing.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id320754","f-b8c1a97c781d"],["q3-3fecd38d3fdf","s3.4-6ac9da9df86c","3.4","Protein Synthesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nactive interpretation of the information coded in a gene to produce a functional gene product","gene expression",["Golgi apparatus","DNA replication","RNA polymerase"],"gene expression: active interpretation of the information coded in a gene to produce a functional gene product.","The answer begins with G and has 2 words.","fs-id2211531","f-58a1f37e4a25"],["q3-cea9e63a4b76","s3.4-005085634062","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnon-coding regions of a pre-mRNA transcript that may be removed during splicing","intron",["promoter","nucleus","codon"],"intron: non-coding regions of a pre-mRNA transcript that may be removed during splicing.","The answer begins with I and has 1 word.","fs-id320754","f-6988331d59f2"],["q3-282f893c0cfb","s3.4-0ac2799726bf","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnucleotide molecule that serves as an intermediate in the genetic code between DNA and protein","messenger RNA (mRNA)",["transfer RNA (tRNA)","ribosomal RNA (rRNA)","nucleus"],"messenger RNA (mRNA): nucleotide molecule that serves as an intermediate in the genetic code between DNA and protein.","The answer begins with M and has 3 words.","fs-id1531263","f-821f02b8809b"],["q3-332096c0143b","s3.4-b045bba3839c","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nchain of amino acids linked by peptide bonds","polypeptide",["promoter","cytoplasm","transcription"],"polypeptide: chain of amino acids linked by peptide bonds.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id864610","f-318fa24a02b5"],["q3-ce107f2fbd26","s3.4-75ea0ceca31a","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsimultaneous translation of a single mRNA transcript by multiple ribosomes","polyribosome",["codon","intron","splicing"],"polyribosome: simultaneous translation of a single mRNA transcript by multiple ribosomes.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id813550","f-a82b863ce179"],["q3-094d93a3c004","s3.4-6501588c9ee6","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregion of DNA that signals transcription to begin at that site within the gene","promoter",["ribosome","proteome","nucleus"],"promoter: region of DNA that signals transcription to begin at that site within the gene.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1864318","f-1092ae625e1c"],["q3-6dbcd271d4c9","s3.4-152cfb3f9c42","3.4","Protein Synthesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfull complement of proteins produced by a cell (determined by the cell’s specific gene expression)","proteome",["anticodon","exon","splicing"],"proteome: full complement of proteins produced by a cell (determined by the cell’s specific gene expression).","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id2211531","f-ed2d32beb9b8"],["q3-e92b47d1d857","s3.4-abc7267c0cef","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nRNA that makes up the subunits of a ribosome","ribosomal RNA (rRNA)",["messenger RNA (mRNA)","transfer RNA (tRNA)","exon"],"ribosomal RNA (rRNA): RNA that makes up the subunits of a ribosome.","The answer begins with R and has 3 words.","fs-id1404772","f-469f829b413b"],["q3-e68f2cf19a3c","s3.4-2d172411d7f3","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nenzyme that unwinds DNA and then adds new nucleotides to a growing strand of RNA for the transcription phase of protein synthesis","RNA polymerase",["DNA replication","Golgi apparatus","gene expression"],"RNA polymerase: enzyme that unwinds DNA and then adds new nucleotides to a growing strand of RNA for the transcription phase of protein synthesis.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id2166515","f-0c6dd04ba297"],["q3-4ac5ba3be19d","s3.4-dd0cd9ce7bb3","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncomplex of enzymes that serves to splice out the introns of a pre-mRNA transcript","spliceosome",["exon","splicing","polypeptide"],"spliceosome: complex of enzymes that serves to splice out the introns of a pre-mRNA transcript.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id320754","f-f19d923c5fae"],["q3-9dfdb13db9a9","s3.4-2d4ce3195d8e","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthe process of modifying a pre-mRNA transcript by removing certain, typically non-coding, regions","splicing",["promoter","triplet","anticodon"],"splicing: the process of modifying a pre-mRNA transcript by removing certain, typically non-coding, regions.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id320754","f-1eb457af5bba"],["q3-78181fdc079b","s3.4-97968fc912dd","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprocess of producing an mRNA molecule that is complementary to a particular gene of DNA","transcription",["codon","translation","triplet"],"transcription: process of producing an mRNA molecule that is complementary to a particular gene of DNA.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1751681","f-5592f6397033"],["q3-5d43890a82eb","s3.4-78e04afd0d01","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmolecules of RNA that serve to bring amino acids to a growing polypeptide strand and properly place them into the sequence","transfer RNA (tRNA)",["messenger RNA (mRNA)","ribosomal RNA (rRNA)","RNA polymerase"],"transfer RNA (tRNA): molecules of RNA that serve to bring amino acids to a growing polypeptide strand and properly place them into the sequence.","The answer begins with T and has 3 words.","fs-id1205938","f-ad8d4f3091a7"],["q3-2e4ddc776ca4","s3.4-2343f08c2d7f","3.4","Protein Synthesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nconsecutive sequence of three nucleotides on a DNA molecule that, when transcribed into an mRNA codon, corresponds to a particular amino acid","triplet",["intron","ribosome","proteome"],"triplet: consecutive sequence of three nucleotides on a DNA molecule that, when transcribed into an mRNA codon, corresponds to a particular amino acid.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1955042","f-953e2376f925"],["q3-f7cdfaf5344a","s3.4-bdc8e6790aae","3.4","Protein Synthesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a functional segment of DNA that provides the genetic information necessary to build a protein.","gene",["genome","transcription","spliceosome"],"A gene is a functional segment of DNA that provides the genetic information necessary to build a protein.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2211531","f-09ac334ba9c2"],["q3-750094b22ca7","s3.4-bdc8e6790aae","3.4","Protein Synthesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEach particular ____ provides the code necessary to construct a particular protein.","gene",["exon","proteome","genome"],"Each particular gene provides the code necessary to construct a particular protein.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2211531","f-415ad4a8e41f"],["q3-94a94776e01f","s3.4-6ac9da9df86c","3.4","Protein Synthesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____, which transforms the information coded in a gene to a final gene product, ultimately dictates the structure and function of a cell by determining which proteins are made.","Gene expression",["Golgi apparatus","DNA replication","RNA polymerase"],"Gene expression, which transforms the information coded in a gene to a final gene product, ultimately dictates the structure and function of a cell by determining which proteins are made.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2211531","f-c4251f9a0ffe"],["q3-91684c41e3f0","s3.4-2343f08c2d7f","3.4","Protein Synthesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a section of three DNA bases in a row that codes for a specific amino acid.","triplet",["splicing","translation","gene"],"A triplet is a section of three DNA bases in a row that codes for a specific amino acid.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1955042","f-a8a8f35349a5"],["q3-ccc43c144754","s3.4-2343f08c2d7f","3.4","Protein Synthesis","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe DNA ____ CAC (cytosine, adenine, and cytosine) specifies the amino acid valine.","triplet",["splicing","proteome","exon"],"For example, the DNA triplet CAC (cytosine, adenine, and cytosine) specifies the amino acid valine.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1955042","f-c8cf4d6e0739"],["q3-50efba1be586","s3.4-bdc8e6790aae","3.4","Protein Synthesis","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____, which is composed of multiple triplets in a unique sequence, provides the code to build an entire protein, with multiple amino acids in the proper sequence.","gene",["nucleus","cytoplasm","splicing"],"Therefore, a gene, which is composed of multiple triplets in a unique sequence, provides the code to build an entire protein, with multiple amino acids in the proper sequence.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1955042","f-6ea36c9facc4"],["q3-665a0ce7845b","s3.4-7fa310b1b4ce","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDNA is housed within the nucleus, and protein synthesis takes place in the ____, thus there must be some sort of intermediate messenger that leaves the nucleus and manages protein synthesis.","cytoplasm",["codon","genome","exon"],"DNA is housed within the nucleus, and protein synthesis takes place in the cytoplasm, thus there must be some sort of intermediate messenger that leaves the nucleus and manages protein synthesis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1531263","f-04701bb68b31"],["q3-ae5767bb60b2","s3.4-6ac9da9df86c","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ begins with the process called transcription, which is the synthesis of a strand of mRNA that is complementary to the gene of interest.","Gene expression",["RNA polymerase","Golgi apparatus","DNA replication"],"Gene expression begins with the process called transcription, which is the synthesis of a strand of mRNA that is complementary to the gene of interest.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1751681","f-fcbf6bcf66d9"],["q3-9655048544e2","s3.4-2fbbf4042dd9","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLike ____, there are three stages to transcription: initiation, elongation, and termination.","DNA replication",["gene expression","RNA polymerase","Golgi apparatus"],"Like DNA replication, there are three stages to transcription: initiation, elongation, and termination.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1751681","f-7ad7f973758d"],["q3-ba4f9909f115","s3.4-97968fc912dd","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA region at the beginning of the gene called a promoter —a particular sequence of nucleotides—triggers the start of ____.","transcription",["splicing","cytoplasm","nucleus"],"A region at the beginning of the gene called a promoter —a particular sequence of nucleotides—triggers the start of transcription.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1864318","f-760beb6dd854"],["q3-4f2815f24fb7","s3.4-2d172411d7f3","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTranscription starts when ____ unwinds the DNA segment.","RNA polymerase",["Golgi apparatus","gene expression","DNA replication"],"Transcription starts when RNA polymerase unwinds the DNA segment.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2166515","f-001c7a09accd"],["q3-a50d9832c34f","s3.4-2d172411d7f3","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is an enzyme that adds new nucleotides to a growing strand of RNA.","RNA polymerase",["Golgi apparatus","gene expression","DNA replication"],"RNA polymerase is an enzyme that adds new nucleotides to a growing strand of RNA.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2166515","f-5a6523e04982"],["q3-47d6b988f804","s3.4-bdc8e6790aae","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAt the end of the ____, a sequence of nucleotides called the terminator sequence causes the new RNA to fold up on itself.","gene",["exon","spliceosome","nucleus"],"At the end of the gene, a sequence of nucleotides called the terminator sequence causes the new RNA to fold up on itself.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1513858","f-46f603f9fbb8"],["q3-5e2383eb8c8d","s3.4-dd0cd9ce7bb3","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ —a structure composed of various proteins and other molecules—attaches to the mRNA and “splices” or cuts out the non-coding regions.","spliceosome",["intron","proteome","gene"],"A spliceosome —a structure composed of various proteins and other molecules—attaches to the mRNA and “splices” or cuts out the non-coding regions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id320754","f-1abf67764d81"],["q3-83a50af97962","s3.4-005085634062","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe removed segment of the transcript is called an ____.","intron",["polyribosome","proteome","polypeptide"],"The removed segment of the transcript is called an intron.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id320754","f-08cf1043839d"],["q3-46ac74203897","s3.4-2d4ce3195d8e","3.4","From DNA to RNA: Transcription","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn exon is a segment of RNA that remains after ____.","splicing",["polyribosome","polypeptide","gene"],"An exon is a segment of RNA that remains after splicing.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id320754","f-81e31d618581"],["q3-b002dbc1e265","s3.4-efd7fd7f2954","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the process of synthesizing a chain of amino acids called a polypeptide.","Translation",["genome","cytoplasm","anticodon"],"Translation is the process of synthesizing a chain of amino acids called a polypeptide.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id864610","f-81ae12eb07d0"],["q3-26b26dc32ecf","s3.4-efd7fd7f2954","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe substrate on which ____ takes place is the ribosome.","translation",["exon","promoter","intron"],"The substrate on which translation takes place is the ribosome.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id864610","f-0672dcf02f7a"],["q3-063934220000","s3.4-a5db44d5ad2e","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nRemember that many of a cell’s ribosomes are found associated with the rough ER, and carry out the synthesis of proteins destined for the ____.","Golgi apparatus",["gene expression","RNA polymerase","DNA replication"],"Remember that many of a cell’s ribosomes are found associated with the rough ER, and carry out the synthesis of proteins destined for the Golgi apparatus.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1404772","f-821c059f52b8"],["q3-de0102cc4ce1","s3.4-abc7267c0cef","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a type of RNA that, together with proteins, composes the structure of the ribosome.","ribosomal RNA (rRNA)",["transfer RNA (tRNA)","messenger RNA (mRNA)","RNA polymerase"],"Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) is a type of RNA that, together with proteins, composes the structure of the ribosome.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1404772","f-7ce97bf6e6dc"],["q3-cd6cf4ccfab6","s3.4-7fa310b1b4ce","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nRibosomes exist in the ____ as two distinct components, a small and a large subunit.","cytoplasm",["promoter","spliceosome","nucleus"],"Ribosomes exist in the cytoplasm as two distinct components, a small and a large subunit.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1404772","f-eb817cca4971"],["q3-2aca7dc45498","s3.4-78e04afd0d01","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a type of RNA that ferries the appropriate corresponding amino acids to the ribosome, and attaches each new amino acid to the last, building the polypeptide chain one-by-one.","transfer RNA (tRNA)",["ribosomal RNA (rRNA)","messenger RNA (mRNA)","intron"],"Transfer RNA (tRNA) is a type of RNA that ferries the appropriate corresponding amino acids to the ribosome, and attaches each new amino acid to the last, building the polypeptide chain one-by-one.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1205938","f-cd0c54f066ed"],["q3-e37623cd7325","s3.4-b045bba3839c","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThus tRNA transfers specific amino acids from the cytoplasm to a growing ____.","polypeptide",["exon","codon","intron"],"Thus tRNA transfers specific amino acids from the cytoplasm to a growing polypeptide.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1205938","f-262e636ac396"],["q3-4ffca83b82ea","s3.4-2fbbf4042dd9","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMuch like the processes of ____ and transcription, translation consists of three main stages: initiation, elongation, and termination.","DNA replication",["gene expression","RNA polymerase","Golgi apparatus"],"Much like the processes of DNA replication and transcription, translation consists of three main stages: initiation, elongation, and termination.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1531123","f-b8b32378aff2"],["q3-bc3a3f57c612","s3.4-018b81a727a4","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nInitiation takes place with the binding of a ____ to an mRNA transcript.","ribosome",["anticodon","exon","gene"],"Initiation takes place with the binding of a ribosome to an mRNA transcript.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1531123","f-1356e63a1525"],["q3-bde0577d9b5c","s3.4-bdc8e6790aae","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThus, a ____ within the DNA molecule is transcribed into mRNA, which is then translated into a protein product.","gene",["codon","intron","spliceosome"],"Thus, a gene within the DNA molecule is transcribed into mRNA, which is then translated into a protein product.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1531123","f-f891d06040b4"],["q3-f9dcdc2298be","s3.4-97968fc912dd","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCommonly, an mRNA ____ will be translated simultaneously by several adjacent ribosomes.","transcription",["translation","proteome","intron"],"Commonly, an mRNA transcription will be translated simultaneously by several adjacent ribosomes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id813550","f-f1690ae49a77"],["q3-9d4d16364bac","s3.4-018b81a727a4","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA single ____ might translate an mRNA molecule in approximately one minute; so multiple ribosomes aboard a single transcript could produce multiple times the number of the same protein in the same minute.","ribosome",["cytoplasm","splicing","exon"],"A single ribosome might translate an mRNA molecule in approximately one minute; so multiple ribosomes aboard a single transcript could produce multiple times the number of the same protein in the same minute.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id813550","f-3ea513bd404c"],["q3-b43524d97d8b","s3.4-75ea0ceca31a","3.4","From RNA to Protein: Translation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a string of ribosomes translating a single mRNA strand.","polyribosome",["codon","triplet","intron"],"A polyribosome is a string of ribosomes translating a single mRNA strand.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id813550","f-f5063dab7dae"],["q3-26d8e3028f5d","s3.5-c10768e89db8","3.5","The Structure of Chromosomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregion of attachment for two sister chromatids","centromere",["telophase","homologous","chromatin"],"centromere: region of attachment for two sister chromatids.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1533532","f-1c3fd351e7a0"],["q3-5ef43def8b07","s3.5-0e4b7f0cac24","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncellular structure that organizes microtubules during cell division","centrosome",["mitosis","kinetochore","centromere"],"centrosome: cellular structure that organizes microtubules during cell division.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1212990","f-867c067ca565"],["q3-58dc8d0d2af5","s3.5-5c528ebc85c1","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprogress point in the cell cycle during which certain conditions must be met in order for the cell to proceed to a subsequence phase","checkpoint",["anaphase","kinetochore","chromatin"],"checkpoint: progress point in the cell cycle during which certain conditions must be met in order for the cell to proceed to a subsequence phase.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1146438","f-872a7a15b30b"],["q3-48983dbc0e6d","s3.5-3e28037831be","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of a group of proteins that function in the progression of the cell cycle","cyclin",["nucleus","mitosis","mutation"],"cyclin: one of a group of proteins that function in the progression of the cell cycle.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1146438","f-a68c3d4b6fbe"],["q3-2313c4e9128c","s3.5-a608355a3244","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of a group of enzymes associated with cyclins that help them perform their functions","cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)",["G 0 phase","G 1 phase","G 2 phase"],"cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK): one of a group of enzymes associated with cyclins that help them perform their functions.","The answer begins with C and has 3 words.","fs-id1146438","f-da2054f3c411"],["q3-4de379aeee4d","s3.5-683c83663cbb","3.5","The Cell Cycle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfinal stage in cell division, where the cytoplasm divides to form two separate daughter cells","cytokinesis",["centromere","homologous","prophase"],"cytokinesis: final stage in cell division, where the cytoplasm divides to form two separate daughter cells.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2127824","f-44f5b0011e16"],["q3-689fc54b71df","s3.5-fedd79c97769","3.5","Cell Growth and Division","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncondition marked by the presence of a double complement of genetic material (two sets of chromosomes, one set inherited from each of two parents)","diploid",["nucleolus","mitosis","cytokinesis"],"diploid: condition marked by the presence of a double complement of genetic material (two sets of chromosomes, one set inherited from each of two parents).","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1282569","f-6b3d21b9b89e"],["q3-c6c2b1a534ff","s3.5-d97fb0124535","3.5","Interphase","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nphase of the cell cycle, usually entered from the G 1 phase; characterized by long or permanent periods where the cell does not move forward into the DNA synthesis phase","G 0 phase",["cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)","G 2 phase","gene"],"G 0 phase: phase of the cell cycle, usually entered from the G 1 phase; characterized by long or permanent periods where the cell does not move forward into the DNA synthesis phase.","The answer begins with G and has 3 words.","fs-id787711","f-4bd901de8887"],["q3-1253431cd2b6","s3.5-ebd7fa21802c","3.5","Interphase","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfirst phase of the cell cycle, after a new cell is born","G 1 phase",["G 2 phase","G 0 phase","cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)"],"G 1 phase: first phase of the cell cycle, after a new cell is born.","The answer begins with G and has 3 words.","fs-id1164738","f-2da9ba5d51a7"],["q3-14bfb39010de","s3.5-7cf7d4e91bdf","3.5","Interphase","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthird phase of the cell cycle, after the DNA synthesis phase","G 2 phase",["G 0 phase","G 1 phase","cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)"],"G 2 phase: third phase of the cell cycle, after the DNA synthesis phase.","The answer begins with G and has 3 words.","fs-id787711","f-cb8c92b225a4"],["q3-10541ce8cb1a","s3.5-f30aba9a456e","3.5","Cell Growth and Division","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes two copies of the same chromosome (not identical), one inherited from each parent","homologous",["anaphase","metaphase","interphase"],"homologous: describes two copies of the same chromosome (not identical), one inherited from each parent.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1282569","f-b670f0962b67"],["q3-a19176c42d53","s3.5-245dd2ec44c3","3.5","The Cell Cycle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nentire life cycle of a cell, excluding mitosis","interphase",["chromatin","cytokinesis","prophase"],"interphase: entire life cycle of a cell, excluding mitosis.","The answer begins with I and has 1 word.","fs-id2127824","f-44f6cfd4a7f7"],["q3-4b56b7031cf1","s3.5-447314345f0c","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregion of a centromere where microtubules attach to a pair of sister chromatids","kinetochore",["prophase","checkpoint","telophase"],"kinetochore: region of a centromere where microtubules attach to a pair of sister chromatids.","The answer begins with K and has 1 word.","fs-id1864386","f-7f0609821c41"],["q3-815722351980","s3.5-84db988b190d","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsecond stage of mitosis (and meiosis), characterized by the linear alignment of sister chromatids in the center of the cell","metaphase",["chromatin","kinetochore","prophase"],"metaphase: second stage of mitosis (and meiosis), characterized by the linear alignment of sister chromatids in the center of the cell.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1283666","f-6f76d78d6b9d"],["q3-f8353161fa31","s3.5-62f32fac0443","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlinear alignment of sister chromatids in the center of the cell, which takes place during metaphase","metaphase plate",["somatic cell","mitotic phase","nuclear envelope"],"metaphase plate: linear alignment of sister chromatids in the center of the cell, which takes place during metaphase.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id1025643","f-53a05f446f9e"],["q3-8d81f638578b","s3.5-fef31ee145ea","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nphase of the cell cycle in which a cell undergoes mitosis","mitotic phase",["stem cell","mitotic spindle","cleavage furrow"],"mitotic phase: phase of the cell cycle in which a cell undergoes mitosis.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id1283666","f-2ac8520ab94f"],["q3-cc24bd87c953","s3.5-8418a0b4d453","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnetwork of microtubules, originating from centrioles, that arranges and pulls apart chromosomes during mitosis","mitotic spindle",["DNA replication","somatic cell","mitotic phase"],"mitotic spindle: network of microtubules, originating from centrioles, that arranges and pulls apart chromosomes during mitosis.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id1212990","f-f4fcbf88ff51"],["q3-a7566ad13129","s3.5-eaf659484b80","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfirst stage of mitosis (and meiosis), characterized by breakdown of the nuclear envelope and condensing of the chromatin to form chromosomes","prophase",["cytokinesis","metaphase","telophase"],"prophase: first stage of mitosis (and meiosis), characterized by breakdown of the nuclear envelope and condensing of the chromatin to form chromosomes.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1283666","f-9af88aeba90d"],["q3-8521d09db5d0","s3.5-ee94d99a7832","3.5","Interphase","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstage of the cell cycle during which DNA replication occurs","S phase",["somatic cell","nuclear envelope","mitotic phase"],"S phase: stage of the cell cycle during which DNA replication occurs.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1164738","f-66a4fa35ad7a"],["q3-7fb67ea2538c","s3.5-fcc23e5fe311","3.5","The Structure of Chromosomes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of a pair of identical chromosomes, formed during DNA replication","sister chromatid",["cleavage furrow","stem cell","metaphase plate"],"sister chromatid: one of a pair of identical chromosomes, formed during DNA replication.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1533532","f-2abaa7346fed"],["q3-430c1220fc00","s3.5-68c4415f3978","3.5","Cell Growth and Division","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nall cells of the body excluding gamete cells","somatic cell",["nuclear envelope","stem cell","cell cycle"],"somatic cell: all cells of the body excluding gamete cells.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1282569","f-3b65d961bd83"],["q3-a148d2ae192d","s3.5-55a5c69f99ea","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncell that is oligo-, multi-, or pleuripotent that has the ability to produce additional stem cells rather than becoming further specialized","stem cell",["mitotic spindle","DNA replication","somatic cell"],"stem cell: cell that is oligo-, multi-, or pleuripotent that has the ability to produce additional stem cells rather than becoming further specialized.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2209015","f-fd3ca36ee23f"],["q3-3bb4fc2b2413","s3.5-9831d756aacc","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfinal stage of mitosis (and meiosis), preceding cytokinesis, characterized by the formation of two new daughter nuclei","telophase",["chromatin","mutation","kinetochore"],"telophase: final stage of mitosis (and meiosis), preceding cytokinesis, characterized by the formation of two new daughter nuclei.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1283666","f-aa69a699f1f5"],["q3-908eec71f92f","s3.5-68c4415f3978","3.5","Cell Growth and Division","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a general term for a body cell, and all human cells, except for the cells that produce eggs and sperm (which are referred to as germ cells), are somatic cells.","somatic cell",["metaphase plate","DNA replication","cleavage furrow"],"A somatic cell is a general term for a body cell, and all human cells, except for the cells that produce eggs and sperm (which are referred to as germ cells), are somatic cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282569","f-d700d2647a8c"],["q3-933b7b051f5b","s3.5-68c4415f3978","3.5","Cell Growth and Division","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA homologous pair of chromosomes is the two copies of a single chromosome found in each ____.","somatic cell",["sister chromatid","mitotic spindle","cleavage furrow"],"A homologous pair of chromosomes is the two copies of a single chromosome found in each somatic cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282569","f-88700bf7a710"],["q3-3b052df4721c","s3.5-fedd79c97769","3.5","Cell Growth and Division","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe human is a ____ organism, having 23 pairs of chromosomes in each of the somatic cells.","diploid",["cytokinesis","metaphase","chromosome"],"The human is a diploid organism, having 23 pairs of chromosomes in each of the somatic cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282569","f-835c377cb910"],["q3-2af599d608dd","s3.5-7b2e10ac92c5","3.5","Cell Growth and Division","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe cells lining the gastrointestinal ____ must be frequently replaced when constantly “worn off” by the movement of food through the gut.","tract",["mitosis","chromatin","cytokinesis"],"For example, the cells lining the gastrointestinal tract must be frequently replaced when constantly “worn off” by the movement of food through the gut.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2248915","f-4c850b7d26b8"],["q3-bb6c2ba764aa","s3.5-58a5049f49f8","3.5","The Cell Cycle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOne “turn” or cycle of the ____ consists of two general phases: interphase, followed by mitosis and cytokinesis.","cell cycle",["mitotic phase","metaphase plate","mitotic spindle"],"One “turn” or cycle of the cell cycle consists of two general phases: interphase, followed by mitosis and cytokinesis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2127824","f-caa8c8dbda06"],["q3-401d76e0eeb0","s3.5-58a5049f49f8","3.5","The Cell Cycle","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nInterphase is the period of the ____ during which the cell is not dividing.","cell cycle",["stem cell","DNA replication","sister chromatid"],"Interphase is the period of the cell cycle during which the cell is not dividing.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2127824","f-5cfad4e63d37"],["q3-4c9043b0cfc4","s3.5-245dd2ec44c3","3.5","The Cell Cycle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe majority of cells are in ____ most of the time.","interphase",["cytoplasm","chromatin","cytokinesis"],"The majority of cells are in interphase most of the time.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2127824","f-81ba48534c46"],["q3-4bb17d64b062","s3.5-4d7fb425b8b1","3.5","The Cell Cycle","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the division of genetic material, during which the cell nucleus breaks down and two new, fully functional, nuclei are formed.","Mitosis",["interphase","chromosome","nucleolus"],"Mitosis is the division of genetic material, during which the cell nucleus breaks down and two new, fully functional, nuclei are formed.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2127824","f-249bfe60d1d7"],["q3-0d7bd94bb92d","s3.5-683c83663cbb","3.5","The Cell Cycle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ divides the cytoplasm into two distinctive cells.","Cytokinesis",["chromosome","checkpoint","telophase"],"Cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm into two distinctive cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2127824","f-aa671d27dc0f"],["q3-dab2e2eb2078","s3.5-ebd7fa21802c","3.5","Interphase","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ (gap 1 phase) is the first gap, or growth phase in the cell cycle.","G 1 phase",["G 2 phase","cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)","G 0 phase"],"G 1 phase (gap 1 phase) is the first gap, or growth phase in the cell cycle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1164738","f-1d29a5c8cc4f"],["q3-c1c71924a38c","s3.5-ee94d99a7832","3.5","Interphase","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFor cells that will divide again, G 1 is followed by replication of the DNA, during the ____.","S phase",["nuclear envelope","sister chromatid","DNA replication"],"For cells that will divide again, G 1 is followed by replication of the DNA, during the S phase.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1164738","f-b3b1421f64ab"],["q3-74aceeaff546","s3.5-7cf7d4e91bdf","3.5","Interphase","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAfter the synthesis phase, the cell proceeds through the ____.","G 2 phase",["cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)","G 1 phase","G 0 phase"],"After the synthesis phase, the cell proceeds through the G 2 phase.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id787711","f-8ab7f811a56f"],["q3-a10082ca5b2d","s3.5-7cf7d4e91bdf","3.5","Interphase","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is a second gap phase, during which the cell continues to grow and makes the necessary preparations for mitosis.","G 2 phase",["cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)","G 0 phase","G 1 phase"],"The G 2 phase is a second gap phase, during which the cell continues to grow and makes the necessary preparations for mitosis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id787711","f-3e660b16bc1f"],["q3-ad564d289694","s3.5-7cf7d4e91bdf","3.5","Interphase","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe S phase typically lasts between 8-10 hours and the ____ approximately 5 hours.","G 2 phase",["G 1 phase","G 0 phase","cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)"],"The S phase typically lasts between 8-10 hours and the G 2 phase approximately 5 hours.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id787711","f-373377b1cc88"],["q3-40c0d85aa061","s3.5-245dd2ec44c3","3.5","The Structure of Chromosomes","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDuring the synthesis phase (S, for DNA synthesis) of ____, the amount of DNA within the cell precisely doubles.","interphase",["homologous","telophase","nucleus"],"During the synthesis phase (S, for DNA synthesis) of interphase, the amount of DNA within the cell precisely doubles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1533532","f-21c283beb059"],["q3-0843be9599ab","s3.5-2fbbf4042dd9","3.5","The Structure of Chromosomes","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAfter ____ but before cell division, each cell actually contains two copies of each chromosome.","DNA replication",["cell cycle","S phase","mitotic phase"],"Therefore, after DNA replication but before cell division, each cell actually contains two copies of each chromosome.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1533532","f-d35b0eb89168"],["q3-2d7b4e13f04a","s3.5-fef31ee145ea","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ of the cell typically takes between 1 and 2 hours.","mitotic phase",["stem cell","cleavage furrow","DNA replication"],"The mitotic phase of the cell typically takes between 1 and 2 hours.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1283666","f-e8f71d272abc"],["q3-39100e7c4ca6","s3.5-683c83663cbb","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ then occurs, dividing the cytoplasm and cell body into two new cells.","Cytokinesis",["prophase","homologous","interphase"],"Cytokinesis then occurs, dividing the cytoplasm and cell body into two new cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1283666","f-6cae1e01bc2f"],["q3-7717a71f1c49","s3.5-acbde35ce843","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nProphase is the first phase of mitosis, during which the loosely packed ____ coils and condenses into visible chromosomes.","chromatin",["telophase","chromosome","nucleus"],"Prophase is the first phase of mitosis, during which the loosely packed chromatin coils and condenses into visible chromosomes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1284144","f-265557917583"],["q3-e315a98d682e","s3.5-c2e472ad718a","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA major occurrence during prophase concerns a very important structure that contains the origin site for ____ growth.","microtubule",["diploid","cytoplasm","mutation"],"A major occurrence during prophase concerns a very important structure that contains the origin site for microtubule growth.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1212990","f-cda85ea2114f"],["q3-6a12818cac37","s3.5-0e4b7f0cac24","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a pair of centrioles together.","centrosome",["kinetochore","homologous","metaphase"],"A centrosome is a pair of centrioles together.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1212990","f-c9852b331628"],["q3-9893434a22ac","s3.5-eaf659484b80","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe cell contains two centrosomes side-by-side, which begin to move apart during ____.","prophase",["microtubule","centrosome","checkpoint"],"The cell contains two centrosomes side-by-side, which begin to move apart during prophase.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1212990","f-8885d0986b85"],["q3-f7fc9dc7923d","s3.5-8418a0b4d453","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNear the end of prophase there is an invasion of the nuclear area by microtubules from the ____.","mitotic spindle",["sister chromatid","stem cell","S phase"],"Near the end of prophase there is an invasion of the nuclear area by microtubules from the mitotic spindle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1864386","f-26da0f807426"],["q3-43119e5d4322","s3.5-8418a0b4d453","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe kinetochore is a protein structure on the centromere that is the point of attachment between the ____ and the sister chromatids.","mitotic spindle",["DNA replication","sister chromatid","S phase"],"The kinetochore is a protein structure on the centromere that is the point of attachment between the mitotic spindle and the sister chromatids.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1864386","f-05442841912e"],["q3-fe18fe81802b","s3.5-62f32fac0443","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ forms between the centrosomes that are now located at either end of the cell.","metaphase plate",["DNA replication","cell cycle","mitotic spindle"],"A metaphase plate forms between the centrosomes that are now located at either end of the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1025643","f-6267aba79496"],["q3-3171635fd6ca","s3.5-62f32fac0443","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the name for the plane through the center of the spindle on which the sister chromatids are positioned.","metaphase plate",["mitotic phase","stem cell","sister chromatid"],"The metaphase plate is the name for the plane through the center of the spindle on which the sister chromatids are positioned.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1025643","f-880c67bebbe2"],["q3-98eab05c6900","s3.5-9831d756aacc","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is characterized by the formation of two new daughter nuclei at either end of the dividing cell.","Telophase",["nucleus","checkpoint","gene"],"Telophase is characterized by the formation of two new daughter nuclei at either end of the dividing cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1804332","f-94c2b21d69c3"],["q3-1df5b889de76","s3.5-2400d247aa59","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is a contractile band made up of microfilaments that forms around the midline of the cell during cytokinesis.","cleavage furrow",["mitotic spindle","cell cycle","stem cell"],"The cleavage furrow is a contractile band made up of microfilaments that forms around the midline of the cell during cytokinesis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2209015","f-c5a15890ec88"],["q3-2c346382452b","s3.5-683c83663cbb","3.5","Mitosis and Cytokinesis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nImagine a cell that completed mitosis but never underwent ____.","cytokinesis",["checkpoint","interphase","centrosome"],"Imagine a cell that completed mitosis but never underwent cytokinesis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1170716","f-76e3d8eb4845"],["q3-e63bf22e275b","s3.5-58a5049f49f8","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA checkpoint is a point in the ____ at which the cycle can be signaled to move forward or stopped.","cell cycle",["cleavage furrow","metaphase plate","somatic cell"],"A checkpoint is a point in the cell cycle at which the cycle can be signaled to move forward or stopped.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1146438","f-fe9030cef194"],["q3-319e37973526","s3.5-58a5049f49f8","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA cyclin is one of the primary classes of ____ control molecules.","cell cycle",["mitotic spindle","somatic cell","metaphase plate"],"A cyclin is one of the primary classes of cell cycle control molecules.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1146438","f-24903a7954fc"],["q3-0d4d04f1e281","s3.5-a608355a3244","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is one of a group of molecules that work together with cyclins to determine progression past cell checkpoints.","cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)",["G 1 phase","G 2 phase","G 0 phase"],"A cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) is one of a group of molecules that work together with cyclins to determine progression past cell checkpoints.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1146438","f-f5797541a828"],["q3-8b73b867aea6","s3.5-5c528ebc85c1","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAt the G 1 ____, the cell must be ready for DNA synthesis to occur.","checkpoint",["mitosis","centrosome","cytoplasm"],"At the G 1 checkpoint, the cell must be ready for DNA synthesis to occur.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1146438","f-0b78de74d2a6"],["q3-de09e2461dec","s3.5-5c528ebc85c1","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAt the G 2 ____ the cell must be fully prepared for mitosis.","checkpoint",["chromatin","diploid","cytokinesis"],"At the G 2 checkpoint the cell must be fully prepared for mitosis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1146438","f-e2666eb1e2b2"],["q3-156b4880492d","s3.5-5c528ebc85c1","3.5","Mechanisms of Cell Cycle Control","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEven during mitosis, a crucial stop and go ____ in metaphase ensures that the cell is fully prepared to complete cell division.","checkpoint",["gene","telophase","nucleolus"],"Even during mitosis, a crucial stop and go checkpoint in metaphase ensures that the cell is fully prepared to complete cell division.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1146438","f-432b67ffa8aa"],["q3-ee049896f96c","s3.5-58a5049f49f8","3.5","Cancer Arises from Homeostatic Imbalances","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTypically, mutations or aberrations in a cell’s DNA that compromise normal ____ control systems lead to cancerous tumors.","cell cycle",["nuclear envelope","stem cell","mitotic spindle"],"Typically, mutations or aberrations in a cell’s DNA that compromise normal cell cycle control systems lead to cancerous tumors.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1119555","f-3e168e2148d0"],["q3-4fa9c78637b3","s3.5-58a5049f49f8","3.5","Cancer Arises from Homeostatic Imbalances","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ control is an example of a homeostatic mechanism that maintains proper cell function and health.","cell cycle",["sister chromatid","mitotic spindle","mitotic phase"],"Cell cycle control is an example of a homeostatic mechanism that maintains proper cell function and health.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1119555","f-3b342cf52fc3"],["q3-58c888a1d728","s3.5-2fbbf4042dd9","3.5","Cancer Arises from Homeostatic Imbalances","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nExamples of proto-oncogene products include cell-surface receptors for growth factors, or cell-signaling molecules, two classes of molecules that can promote ____ and cell division.","DNA replication",["cleavage furrow","mitotic phase","S phase"],"Examples of proto-oncogene products include cell-surface receptors for growth factors, or cell-signaling molecules, two classes of molecules that can promote DNA replication and cell division.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2192433","f-c9177f7aa6a9"],["q3-3a9397f778fa","s3.5-58a5049f49f8","3.5","Cancer Arises from Homeostatic Imbalances","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA delicate homeostatic balance between the many proto-oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes delicately controls the ____ and ensures that only healthy cells replicate.","cell cycle",["S phase","sister chromatid","mitotic phase"],"A delicate homeostatic balance between the many proto-oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes delicately controls the cell cycle and ensures that only healthy cells replicate.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1526500","f-ed8380c7f373"],["q3-a045bb2f0da5","s3.6-0fe9a6907671","3.6","Stem Cells","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the condition of being able to differentiate into different types of cells within a given cell lineage or small number of lineages, such as a red blood cell or white blood cell","multipotent",["gene","transcription","unipotent"],"multipotent: describes the condition of being able to differentiate into different types of cells within a given cell lineage or small number of lineages, such as a red blood cell or white blood cell.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id2212334","f-d29d33da9f88"],["q3-18716f680f0b","s3.6-437c5fe5d81a","3.6","Stem Cells","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the condition of being more specialized than multipotency; the condition of being able to differentiate into one of a few possible cell types","oligopotent",["totipotent","multipotent","unipotent"],"oligopotent: describes the condition of being more specialized than multipotency; the condition of being able to differentiate into one of a few possible cell types.","The answer begins with O and has 1 word.","fs-id1942506","f-3a41f7387efa"],["q3-e4476d04d696","s3.6-6e31ac8177b5","3.6","Stem Cells","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the condition of being able to differentiate into a large variety of cell types","pluripotent",["genome","multipotent","oligopotent"],"pluripotent: describes the condition of being able to differentiate into a large variety of cell types.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id2212334","f-db43fa53cf31"],["q3-2011c831c58c","s3.6-362746aa5a91","3.6","Stem Cells","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nembryonic cells that have the ability to differentiate into any type of cell and organ in the body","totipotent",["unipotent","pluripotent","transcription"],"totipotent: embryonic cells that have the ability to differentiate into any type of cell and organ in the body.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id2056386","f-6498134d6f7e"],["q3-f6ddbdac66ab","s3.6-73f7d709b0ba","3.6","Differentiation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of the proteins that regulate the transcription of genes","transcription factor",["RNA polymerase","stem cell","gene expression"],"transcription factor: one of the proteins that regulate the transcription of genes.","The answer begins with T and has 2 words.","fs-id1544098","f-7ddb1029c0f4"],["q3-7e2253c6f9b0","s3.6-a56cf4ebe6ba","3.6","Stem Cells","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the condition of being committed to a single specialized cell type","unipotent",["oligopotent","gene","pluripotent"],"unipotent: describes the condition of being committed to a single specialized cell type.","The answer begins with U and has 1 word.","fs-id1942506","f-857b6d38c1b1"],["q3-d049dce10a8e","s3.6-a0416830681c","3.6","Cellular Differentiation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the process by which unspecialized cells become specialized to carry out distinct functions.","Differentiation",["totipotent","gene","oligopotent"],"Differentiation is the process by which unspecialized cells become specialized to carry out distinct functions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2104336","f-b66eca18f2c7"],["q3-043ebce6a1ae","s3.6-55a5c69f99ea","3.6","Stem Cells","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is an unspecialized cell that can divide without limit as needed and can, under specific conditions, differentiate into specialized cells.","stem cell",["gene expression","RNA polymerase","transcription factor"],"A stem cell is an unspecialized cell that can divide without limit as needed and can, under specific conditions, differentiate into specialized cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2152443","f-ef7bd7539db2"],["q3-f62a74371b17","s3.6-6e31ac8177b5","3.6","Stem Cells","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe embryonic cells that develop from totipotent stem cells and are precursors to the fundamental tissue layers of the embryo are classified as ____.","pluripotent",["oligopotent","unipotent","gene"],"The embryonic cells that develop from totipotent stem cells and are precursors to the fundamental tissue layers of the embryo are classified as pluripotent.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2212334","f-0c81d8f37700"],["q3-b973060f5778","s3.6-55a5c69f99ea","3.6","Stem Cells","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA pluripotent ____ is one that has the potential to differentiate into any type of human tissue but cannot support the full development of an organism.","stem cell",["RNA polymerase","gene expression","transcription factor"],"A pluripotent stem cell is one that has the potential to differentiate into any type of human tissue but cannot support the full development of an organism.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2212334","f-78f28d7bc209"],["q3-8d8c9b865808","s3.6-55a5c69f99ea","3.6","Stem Cells","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn oligopotent ____ is limited to becoming one of a few different cell types.","stem cell",["gene expression","RNA polymerase","transcription factor"],"An oligopotent stem cell is limited to becoming one of a few different cell types.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1942506","f-8b7198eb067a"],["q3-8421f55099f9","s3.6-97968fc912dd","3.6","Differentiation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe primary mechanism by which genes are turned “on” or “off” is through ____ factors.","transcription",["oligopotent","genome","unipotent"],"The primary mechanism by which genes are turned “on” or “off” is through transcription factors.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1544098","f-9377d6788b43"],["q3-54fa06162c64","s3.6-55a5c69f99ea","3.6","Stem Cell Research","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ research aims to find ways to use stem cells to regenerate and repair cellular damage.","stem cell",["transcription factor","RNA polymerase","gene expression"],"Stem cell research aims to find ways to use stem cells to regenerate and repair cellular damage.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1332564","f-902a8484835a"],["q3-3e2195269a84","s3.6-6e31ac8177b5","3.6","Stem Cell Research","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nHuman embryonic stem cells (hESCs) are extracted from embryos and are ____.","pluripotent",["transcription","gene","unipotent"],"Human embryonic stem cells (hESCs) are extracted from embryos and are pluripotent.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1534313","f-93e8a6b1e1e7"],["q3-53a067589dd4","s3.6-0fe9a6907671","3.6","Stem Cell Research","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe stem cells isolated from umbilical cord blood are also ____, as are cells from deciduous teeth (baby teeth).","multipotent",["genome","transcription","totipotent"],"The stem cells isolated from umbilical cord blood are also multipotent, as are cells from deciduous teeth (baby teeth).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1534313","f-13c4cd90064f"],["q3-7f4fe653a940","s3.6-6e31ac8177b5","3.6","Stem Cell Research","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nResearchers have recently developed induced ____ stem cells (iPSCs) from mouse and human adult stem cells.","pluripotent",["transcription","totipotent","oligopotent"],"Researchers have recently developed induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) from mouse and human adult stem cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1534313","f-713af2184f63"],["q3-edit-c8fef3cecd23","s3.2-edit-a0804283efc0","3.2","Organelles for Energy Production and Detoxification","connections","Which cell type carries out extensive detoxification in the liver?","Hepatocytes",["Erythrocytes","Osteocytes","Photoreceptors"],"Hepatocytes perform many liver functions, including processing and detoxifying substances.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1988147","f-120f5580d7ff"],["q3-edit-497559bdc4ea","s3.2-edit-2385d7d2ec0a","3.2","Cell: The Free Radical Theory","connections","What does the free radical theory of aging propose?","Accumulated oxidative damage contributes to age-related changes",["Free radicals are always harmless nutrients","Every aging change results only from bone growth","Cells become immune to oxidative damage with age"],"The theory links accumulated oxidative damage with aspects of aging; it does not establish one universal cause of all aging.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1530809","f-d3ce7a7daad3"],["q3-edit-ba8851aa165e","s3.5-edit-c6c6d13422c2","3.5","The Cell Cycle Out of Control: Implications","connections","How can failure of cell-cycle controls contribute to cancer?","It can permit unwanted, excessive cell division",["It ensures that damaged cells never divide","It converts all dividing cells into mature neurons","It makes DNA unnecessary for cell replication"],"Cell-cycle checkpoints normally help regulate division; their failure can allow abnormal proliferation.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1476740","f-674114c2add5"],["q3-edit-2204e8044216","s3.5-edit-5f18c00c1b50","3.5","Cell Cycle Control","connections","What can a cell-cycle checkpoint do when DNA is damaged?","Pause cell-cycle progression so damage can be addressed",["Force immediate division before DNA replication","Remove the need for chromosome duplication","Convert replicated chromosomes into ribosomes"],"Cell-cycle checkpoints regulate progression and can stop a damaged cell from proceeding toward division.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1885543","f-5595c4a56065"]]);
